const helpTopics = <String, String>{
  "admin-app": "# itcmdr Admin app\n\nThe app for owners, admins and SREs who look after many sites through [adminsvr](ADMINSVR.md)\n(Linux, macOS, Windows, Android; iOS builds but needs signing). It uses the same signed client as\nthe [admin CLI](ADMIN-CLI.md).\n\n1. Enroll the device with a one-time code from an owner or admin (`itcmdr-admin user create`). The\n   app shows your 24 words once and asks three back.\n2. On first contact it checks adminsvr's certificate ([certificates](CERTIFICATES.md)).\n\n| Tab | What |\n|---|---|\n| Alerts | open, resolved or all; acknowledge and resolve; a banner for new alerts while the app is open |\n| Servers | status, configuration, versions per machine (\"update available\"), metrics history (6 h to 30 days), commands, start a backup, last deep backup check; admins add and revoke servers |\n| Plugins | the plugin and release catalog, approvals, the second-approver switch (owners) |\n| Users | (owners and admins) create with a code, roles, disable, SRE scopes, approve and revoke devices, reset passkeys |\n| Settings | passkeys, the step-up policy (owners), notifications, audit verify, the trusted certificate, forget this device, Help |\n\nActions on the step-up list show \"Touch your security key\" while the call waits for the touch. There\nis no web build (the client uses gRPC over TLS sockets).\n",
  "admin-cli": "# itcmdr-admin: the adminsvr CLI\n\n`itcmdr-admin` is the command line client for [adminsvr](ADMINSVR.md). Each device has its own key,\ncertified with your 24 words at enrollment; every request is signed. The identity lives in the user\nconfig folder (`itcmdr-admin`, mode 0600) or `ITCMDR_ADMIN_HOME`. Every command and flag:\n`man itcmdr-admin` ([man pages](MAN.md)).\n\n| Command | What |\n|---|---|\n| `info <addr>`, `trust show\\|review\\|set\\|remove` | server version, certificate mode and expiry; [certificate trust](CERTIFICATES.md) |\n| `enroll <code>`, `recover <addr> ...`, `whoami` | enroll this device (24 words shown once), a new device from the words |\n| `user ...`, `device ...` | people, roles (owner, admin, sre), device approvals |\n| `key-backup on\\|off` | the encrypted key backup (on by default) |\n| `site ...` | create sites (one-time site token), list with status and last deep check, metrics |\n| `alert list\\|ack\\|resolve` | alerts from every site you may see |\n| `backup start\\|drill <site>` | start a backup; the restore drill (ok or failed only) |\n| `command ...`, `freeze on\\|off` | signed commands and their approvals; stop everything (owners) |\n| `config show\\|set`, `schedule ...`, `plugin ...`, `release ...` | a site's settings and schedules, plugins and releases (`--dry-run` previews) |\n| `org policy` | second approver on or off, deep-check limit (owners) |\n| `passkey ...`, `policy ...` | passkeys and the step-up policy (libfido2's tools; `ITCMDR_FIDO2_DEVICE`) |\n| `push register\\|unregister\\|test` | push notifications for this device |\n| `audit list\\|verify` | the hash-chained audit log |\n| `help [topic]` | these guides in the terminal ([in-app help](HELP.md)) |\n",
  "adminsvr": "# adminsvr\n\nThe offsite admin server: one place where owners, admins and SREs see the status and alerts of\nmany sites and send a few safe commands. Design and decisions: [historical/1.0/design/ADMINSVR.md](historical/1.0/design/ADMINSVR.md).\nStep-by-step deployment with Let's Encrypt: [deploying adminsvr](DEPLOY-ADMINSVR.md). The CLI:\n[admin CLI](ADMIN-CLI.md); the app: [admin app](ADMIN-APP.md).\n\n## What leaves a site\n\nThe site server decides, deny by default (`server/src/sitelink.rs`, `Redactor`):\n\n- opaque site-scoped IDs (`m-\u2026` machines, `a-\u2026` alerts), never names;\n- alert codes built from a fixed vocabulary, check ids and plugin names; anything else is hashed;\n- severity, state, counts, timestamps and numeric rollups (machines up/late/down, backup age, size, result);\n- display names only after the owner opts in on the site: `itcmdr-cli adminsvr share-names on`.\n\nHostnames, IPs, paths, usernames and message text never leave; tests in `adminsvr/tests/sitelink.rs`\nand `server/src/sitelink.rs` check this. Backups never leave the site and there is no restore from\nadminsvr.\n\n## Install adminsvr\n\nUses SQLite in its data folder by default (`<data dir>/adminsvr.db`); for Postgres 14+, create a\ndatabase and set `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_DB_URL=postgres://\u2026`. Ports: 8444 admin API, 8443 SiteLink.\nSettings come from flags, the environment or an env file (`--env-file`, `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ENV_FILE`;\nvalues already in the environment win). The service definitions all pass the env file below.\nadminsvr stops cleanly on SIGTERM (every service manager) or Ctrl-C.\n\n### Linux: systemd, SysV init or OpenRC\n\n    tar xzf itcmdr-adminsvr-<ver>-linux-x86_64.tar.gz && cd itcmdr-adminsvr-<ver>-linux-x86_64\n    sudo ./install.sh                 # or --init systemd | openrc | sysv (default: what the system runs)\n    sudo editor /etc/itcmdr-adminsvr/adminsvr.env   # public host:ports, TLS names (and a Postgres URL if wanted)\n    sudo -u itcmdr-adminsvr itcmdr-adminsvr --env-file /etc/itcmdr-adminsvr/adminsvr.env init --email you@example.com --name \"You\"\n\nThen start it with what `install.sh` printed:\n\n| Init | Files | Start / stop / status | Log |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| systemd | `/etc/systemd/system/itcmdr-adminsvr.service` | `systemctl enable --now itcmdr-adminsvr`, `systemctl stop/status itcmdr-adminsvr` | `journalctl -u itcmdr-adminsvr` |\n| SysV | `/etc/init.d/itcmdr-adminsvr` (LSB headers; `update-rc.d` or `chkconfig`), pidfile `/run/itcmdr-adminsvr.pid` | `/etc/init.d/itcmdr-adminsvr start\\|stop\\|restart\\|status` | `/var/log/itcmdr-adminsvr.log` |\n| OpenRC | `/etc/init.d/itcmdr-adminsvr` (`openrc-run`) | `rc-update add itcmdr-adminsvr default; rc-service itcmdr-adminsvr start` | `/var/log/itcmdr-adminsvr.log` |\n\nEvery variant runs as the `itcmdr-adminsvr` system user with its data in `/var/lib/itcmdr-adminsvr`\n(0700). The Linux binary needs the system OpenSSL 3 (`libssl3`, used by the built-in libpq).\nUninstall: `sudo ./uninstall.sh` (keeps data and settings) or `--purge`; a Postgres database is never dropped.\n\n### macOS: launchd\n\n    tar xzf itcmdr-adminsvr-<ver>-macos-aarch64.tar.gz && cd itcmdr-adminsvr-<ver>-macos-aarch64\n    sudo ./install-macos.sh\n    sudo editor /usr/local/etc/itcmdr-adminsvr/adminsvr.env\n    sudo -u _itcmdradminsvr env ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_DATA_DIR=/usr/local/var/itcmdr-adminsvr \\\n      itcmdr-adminsvr --env-file /usr/local/etc/itcmdr-adminsvr/adminsvr.env init --email you@example.com --name \"You\"\n    sudo launchctl bootstrap system /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.weirdbrains.itcmdr-adminsvr.plist\n\nThe LaunchDaemon runs as the hidden `_itcmdradminsvr` account, restarts after a failure\n(`KeepAlive`/`SuccessfulExit=false`), logs to `/usr/local/var/log/itcmdr-adminsvr.log` and keeps\nits data in `/usr/local/var/itcmdr-adminsvr`. Stop: `sudo launchctl bootout system/com.weirdbrains.itcmdr-adminsvr`.\nUninstall: `sudo ./uninstall-macos.sh [--purge]`. The binary is unsigned: if macOS blocks it, run\n`xattr -d com.apple.quarantine itcmdr-adminsvr` before installing.\n\n### Windows: a Windows service\n\n`itcmdr-adminsvr-Setup-<ver>.exe` installs `%ProgramFiles%\\itcmdr\\adminsvr\\itcmdr-adminsvr.exe` and\nthe service **itcmdr-adminsvr** (LocalSystem, automatic start). Recovery: restart after 5 s, 30 s,\nthen every 2 minutes (also when it stops with an error), reset after a day. Settings:\n`%ProgramData%\\itcmdr-adminsvr\\adminsvr.env` (kept on upgrade and uninstall); data (root key, TLS,\nSQLite) in `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr-adminsvr\\data` and the log in `\u2026\\logs`; only Administrators and\nSYSTEM can read that folder. First owner, in an elevated prompt:\n\n    \"%ProgramFiles%\\itcmdr\\adminsvr\\itcmdr-adminsvr.exe\" init --email you@example.com --name \"You\"\n\nSilent: `/VERYSILENT /SUPPRESSMSGBOXES /NORESTART [/NOSTART=yes]`; `sc stop|start itcmdr-adminsvr`.\nUninstalling stops and removes the service and keeps `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr-adminsvr` (the\nuninstaller's `/REMOVEDATA=yes` deletes it). Allow TCP 8443/8444 in the firewall yourself.\n\n### Container (rootless Podman)\n\n    podman build -f packaging/adminsvr/Containerfile -t itcmdr-adminsvr .\n    podman run -d --name itcmdr-adminsvr -p 8443:8443 -p 8444:8444 -v itcmdr-adminsvr:/var/lib/itcmdr-adminsvr \\\n      -e ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_PUBLIC_API=<host>:8444 -e ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_PUBLIC_SITE=<host>:8443 itcmdr-adminsvr\n    podman exec itcmdr-adminsvr itcmdr-adminsvr init --email you@example.com --name \"You\"\n\nOr `podman compose up -d` with `packaging/adminsvr/compose.yaml` (SQLite; `--profile postgres` for\nPostgres). Tested with rootless Podman 6.1: `scripts/container-smoke.sh` runs the image, enrolls\nthe CLI, creates a site, checks the audit chain and that data survives a restart, then exercises\nthe SysV script inside the image.\n\n### Console commands\n\n`init` prints the first owner's enrollment code. `itcmdr-adminsvr recover-owner --email \u2026`\n(console) makes a new owner code when every owner device and the words are lost; it is audited.\n`itcmdr-adminsvr audit-verify` checks the audit chain.\n\n## Admin CLI\n\n    itcmdr-admin enroll <code>        # shows your 24 words once, asks three back; key backup on\n    itcmdr-admin whoami\n    itcmdr-admin user create <email> --name \u2026      # role sre by default; prints their code\n    itcmdr-admin user role <id> admin|sre|owner   # only owners grant or change owner\n    itcmdr-admin user approve <user> <fingerprint> # new devices wait for an owner/admin\n    itcmdr-admin site create <name>               # prints a one-time site token\n    itcmdr-admin alert list | ack | resolve\n    itcmdr-admin backup start <site>\n    itcmdr-admin org policy --deep-check-days 90  # v1.0.0: warn when a site's last deep backup check is older (owners)\n    itcmdr-admin backup drill <site>              # v0.22.0: the site checks its newest backup; you get ok/failed\n    itcmdr-admin audit list | verify\n    itcmdr-admin recover <addr> --pin \u2026 --email \u2026 # new device from the 24 words\n    itcmdr-admin key-backup off                   # opt out of the encrypted key backup\n\nIdentity: the user config folder (`itcmdr-admin`, mode 0600), or `ITCMDR_ADMIN_HOME`.\n\n## Link a site\n\nOn the site server, in person:\n\n    itcmdr-cli adminsvr enroll <site token>\n    itcmdr-cli adminsvr status\n    itcmdr-cli adminsvr allow backup.start backup.drill alert.ack alert.resolve   # local command allowlist\n    itcmdr-cli adminsvr unlink\n\nSetting `adminsvr.enabled=false` closes the link at once (kill switch); `adminsvr.heartbeatSecs`\noverrides the status interval. Every command is checked for both signatures (user and adminsvr\nroot key), expiry, the never-remote list and the local allowlist, and is written to the site audit.\n\n## Commands, configs and plugins (v0.20.0)\n\nA site sends its configuration document (setting values only for numbers, switches and times; paths and text\nstay on the site; schedules by opaque id, names only with the display-name opt-in) and its plugin list. adminsvr\nsends signed commands back; the site checks the user signature, its local allowlist, the never-remote list and,\nfor plugin kinds, the second person's signed approval when the organization requires one (v0.21.0), then audits.\n\n    itcmdr-admin config show <site> [--all]\n    itcmdr-admin config set <site> backup.keep_daily 9 --dry-run     # preview: before/after, nothing changes\n    itcmdr-admin schedule pause <site> <schedule-id>\n    itcmdr-admin plugin upload disk-watch-1.2.0.tar.gz               # owners/admins; signed package\n    itcmdr-admin plugin push <site> <sha256>                          # waits for approval\n    itcmdr-admin command approve <id>                                 # another owner/admin\n    itcmdr-admin freeze on|off                                        # owners: stop everything\n\nOn the site: `itcmdr-cli adminsvr allow setting.set schedule.pause plugin.install ...` (nothing new is allowed by\ndefault). `adminsvr.requireTwoPerson` (default false since v0.21.0) can only be changed on the site: when on,\nthe site always demands a second approver, whatever the organization's setting says.\n\n## One approver or two: the organization setting (v0.21.0)\n\nEvery action that took a second owner or admin in v0.20 (plugin install/enable/disable from adminsvr) and the new\nrelease approvals follow one organization setting, **off by default**: one owner or admin approves.\n\n    itcmdr-admin org policy                                # show it (and who changed it last)\n    itcmdr-admin org policy --second-approver on|off       # owners only; audited (\"org.policy\")\n\nThe setting is not only adminsvr's word: each command carries it inside the requester's signature\n(`two_person`), adminsvr refuses a command whose flag does not match the setting, and the site checks the\napprovals the flag asks for. A compromised adminsvr could send `two_person = false`; a site that wants two\napprovers whatever happens sets its own `adminsvr.requireTwoPerson = true`. With the setting off, a device may\napprove its own request where the flow still shows an approval step. The admin app's Plugins page has the switch\n(owners) and its approval buttons work with one approver.\n\nUpgrade note: an organization that has two or more active owners/admins when adminsvr v0.21.0 first starts keeps\nthe second approver (the migration turns the setting on for it); turn it off with the command above.\n\n## Releases: approve once, adminsvr rolls out (v0.21.0)\n\nServer and agent releases (`itcmdr-server-release` / `itcmdr-agent-release` packages, signed by an owner key as\nbefore) go into the catalog like plugins. An owner or admin approves **one exact release** once: their device key\nsigns `itcmdr-admin-release/v1, name, version, sha256`; with the second approver on, a different owner or admin\nsigns too. adminsvr endorses each approval with its root key and attaches them to every rollout command.\n\n    itcmdr-admin plugin upload itcmdr-agent-release-0.21.0.tar.gz\n    itcmdr-admin release list                              # approvals so far, and how many are needed\n    itcmdr-admin release approve <sha256>\n    itcmdr-admin release rollout <site> <sha256>           # installs in the site's update window\n    itcmdr-admin release rollout <site> <sha256> --now     # push: ignores the window\n\nThe site (after `itcmdr-cli adminsvr allow ... release.rollout`; it is not allowed by default) checks the\napprovals (distinct owner/admin users and keys, endorsed by the root key), fetches the package, checks its\nchecksum and the owner signature on it, installs it and starts the server upgrade or the agent rollout through\nits rings. Rings run within a site; across sites, you choose which sites get it and when.\n\n## Automatic updates and the update window (v0.21.0)\n\nSites update themselves by default (`updates.auto = true`), but only inside their update window\n(`updates.window`, default `daily 01:00-04:00`, in `updates.timezone`, default the server's zone). The window\nholds the server upgrade, agent offers and ring advances; a range like `Mon-Fri 22:00-02:00` runs past midnight\nand belongs to the day it starts; daylight-saving changes follow the zone's wall clock. A bad window fails closed\n(nothing installs) until fixed. Change it on the site (`itcmdr-cli config set updates.window \"Sat,Sun 02:00-05:00\"`,\nthe site app's Updates screen) or from adminsvr (`itcmdr-admin config set <site> updates.window ...`).\n\n- Off per site: `updates.auto = false` (an approved release is installed but waits for a push).\n- Off per machine: `agent.upgrades = false` holds that machine back, even from a push.\n- Push now: `release rollout ... --now` (or `itcmdr-cli upgrade start <v> --now`) ignores the window; the same\n  approval rules apply.\n- Order: the server upgrades first (database safety copy, staged copy, swap by `itcmdr-server-watchdog`, health\n  check, rollback with the database restored on failure, Critical `server-upgrade` alert); agent rollouts that\n  need a newer server (`min_server` in release.toml) wait for it.\n\n## Passkeys / security keys (v0.19.1, optional)\n\nThe 24-word key signs everything; a passkey is a second factor on top. The CLI uses a USB/NFC\nsecurity key through libfido2's tools (`fido2-token`, `fido2-cred`, `fido2-assert`).\n\n    itcmdr-admin passkey add --label yubikey [--unlock]   # --unlock: the device key opens only with a touch\n    itcmdr-admin passkey list [user]\n    itcmdr-admin passkey remove <credential id>\n    itcmdr-admin passkey reset <user> <credential id>     # owner/admin: a user's lost key\n    itcmdr-admin passkey lock | unlock\n    itcmdr-admin policy show\n    itcmdr-admin policy set --stepup SetRole,RevokeDevice,... [--required-roles owner] [--uv]\n\nOnce you have a passkey, actions on the step-up list ask for a touch, bound to that exact request.\n`ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_RP_ID` sets the WebAuthn RP id (default: the host of the public API address);\n`ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_WEB_ORIGINS` adds the web app's origins. A real security key has not been tested\nwith this release yet (the tests use a software authenticator).\n\n## itcmdr Admin app (v0.19.2)\n\nThe app for owners, admins and SREs (Linux, macOS, Windows, Android; iOS builds but needs signing).\nIt uses the same signed client as the CLI (`admin-app/rust` over `itcmdr-admin-cli`): enroll the\ndevice with a one-time code (the 24 words are shown once), then every request is signed with the\ndevice key and adminsvr's TLS key is pinned. Tabs: Alerts (open/resolved/all, acknowledge and\nresolve), Servers (status, configuration, versions per machine with \"update available\", metrics\nhistory for 6 h to 30 days, commands, start a backup; admins add and revoke servers), Users\n(owners/admins: create with a code, roles, disable, SRE scopes, approve and revoke devices, reset\npasskeys) and Settings (passkeys, the step-up policy for owners, notifications, audit verify,\nforget this device). Actions on the step-up list show \"Touch your security key\" while the call\nwaits for the touch; on desktops the app drives the key through libfido2's tools like the CLI.\nChanging a server's settings and updating it remotely come with v0.20/v0.21. There is no web\nbuild: the client uses gRPC over TLS sockets, which browsers do not allow (it needs gRPC-web).\n\nApp ids: `com.weirdbrains.itcmdr_admin` (Android application id, Linux) and\n`com.weirdbrains.itcmdrAdmin` (iOS and macOS bundle id, also the APNs topic). They have not\nchanged since the app was added, so Android and the desktops upgrade in place.\n\n## Push notifications (v0.19.2)\n\nadminsvr pushes a newly opened alert (default: cautious and critical,\n`ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_PUSH_MIN_SEVERITY`) to the registered devices of every active user who may see\nthe site (SREs only for their scoped sites; revoked devices and disabled users get nothing). The\npayload holds only the opaque site id, the opaque alert id and the severity, with a fixed title\n(\"itcmdr: critical alert\"); the app loads the details over the signed API. Every send is audited\n(`push.send`), tokens that the provider reports gone are deleted. `itcmdr-admin push register |\nunregister | test` (the app does the same), `itcmdr-admin site metrics <site>` for the history.\n\nPush is off until configured. What it needs (accounts the operator creates):\n- **Android (FCM)**: a Firebase project with Cloud Messaging; a service account key (JSON) with\n  the Firebase Cloud Messaging API role \u2192 `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=/path/sa.json`; and\n  the app's `google-services.json` for an app build with the Firebase messaging plugin.\n- **iOS/macOS (APNs)**: an Apple Developer team, an APNs auth key (.p8) with its key id and the\n  team id, and the app's bundle id with the Push Notifications capability \u2192\n  `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_APNS_KEY`, `_KEY_ID`, `_TEAM_ID`, `_TOPIC` (and\n  `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_APNS_URL=https://api.sandbox.push.apple.com` for development builds).\n- The released app does not include a push SDK yet (`admin-app/lib/push.dart`: `NoPush`); it shows\n  new alerts in a banner while open (checked every 30 s). The server side is tested against a\n  local FCM/APNs stub (`adminsvr/tests/push.rs`).\n\n## adminsvr's own backup (v1.2.0)\n\nadminsvr backs up its own database, keys, TLS and ACME files and settings, encrypted for its own\nrecovery words, nightly or on demand, with a restore drill and a restore onto a new machine. It\nnever holds or opens site backups. See [adminsvr backup and restore](BACKUP-ADMINSVR.md).\n\n## Deep backup check reminder (v1.0.0)\n\nEach site status carries `deep_check_ms` and `deep_check_result` (ok | failed | none): the date and\nresult of the site's last `itcmdr-cli backup drill --deep`, which only runs on the site with the 24\nwords. Nothing else about backups is sent. `itcmdr-admin site list` and the admin app's Servers page\nshow \"last deep check: N days ago\" and a warning when it never ran, failed, or is older than the\norganization's limit (`OrgPolicy.deep_check_max_days`, default 90, `itcmdr-admin org policy\n--deep-check-days N`, owners only, audited as `org.policy`).\n\n## HTTPS certificates and Let's Encrypt (v1.1.0)\n\nadminsvr always has its **internal certificate** (`<data dir>/tls/server.pem`, issued by its own CA).\nEnrollment codes and site tokens carry its SHA-256, so enrolled devices and sites pin it; the\nSiteLink (sites) only ever uses it, with mTLS. That does not change with ACME.\n\nOptionally the **admin API** also gets a publicly trusted certificate from Let's Encrypt (or any\nACME CA), for browsers, the web app and clients that use the system trust store. It is off by\ndefault and everything is per install (flags, environment or the env file; nothing is built in):\n\n| Setting (flag / env `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_\u2026`) | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `--acme-domains` / `ACME_DOMAINS` | empty = off | certificate names, comma separated (e.g. `admin.example.com`) |\n| `--acme-contact` / `ACME_CONTACT` | none | contact e-mail(s) for the CA account |\n| `--acme-directory` / `ACME_DIRECTORY` | `production` | `production`, `staging` (Let's Encrypt staging) or a directory URL |\n| `--acme-challenge` / `ACME_CHALLENGE` | `tls-alpn-01` | `tls-alpn-01` (on the admin API port; the CA connects to port **443** of each name, so forward 443 to `--api-addr`) or `http-01` (the CA connects to port **80**; served on `--acme-http-addr`) |\n| `--acme-http-addr` / `ACME_HTTP_ADDR` | `0.0.0.0:80` | HTTP-01 listener (only with `http-01`; serves challenge tokens and nothing else) |\n| `--acme-cache-dir` / `ACME_CACHE_DIR` | `<data dir>/acme` | account key and certificate (directory 0700, files 0600) |\n| `--acme-renew-days` / `ACME_RENEW_DAYS` | `30` | renew this many days before expiry (1-60) |\n| `--acme-retry-secs` / `ACME_RETRY_SECS` | `3600` | wait after a failed attempt |\n| `--acme-directory-root` / `ACME_DIRECTORY_ROOT` | none | PEM root for the directory's own HTTPS (test CAs such as Pebble only) |\n\nExample env file:\n\n    ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_DOMAINS=admin.example.com\n    ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_CONTACT=it@example.com\n    ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_DIRECTORY=staging    # try staging first, then production\n\nHow it works:\n- Each handshake picks the certificate by name (SNI): the ACME names get the ACME certificate;\n  IP addresses, other names and enrolled devices (which send a fixed internal name) get the internal\n  one. So enabling ACME does not disturb any enrolled device or site.\n- The first certificate is requested at start. Renewal runs `renew-days` before expiry and the new\n  certificate is served at once (no restart). A failed attempt **keeps serving the current\n  certificate**, records the error and opens the adminsvr alert `tls-acme` (Critical when the\n  certificate expires within 7 days or there is none, Cautious otherwise); the next success\n  resolves it. It retries after `retry-secs`.\n- Status: `itcmdr-adminsvr tls-status` on the server (mode, names, expiry, last renewal, issuer,\n  fingerprint, last error) and `itcmdr-admin info <addr>` anywhere (mode, names, expiry, a warning\n  when the last renewal failed; errors are not shown over the network).\n- Tests run against Pebble (Let's Encrypt's test CA) in podman: `adminsvr/tests/tls.rs`\n  (`podman pull ghcr.io/letsencrypt/pebble ghcr.io/letsencrypt/pebble-challtestsrv`; the test is\n  skipped without them or with `ITCMDR_SKIP_PEBBLE=1`).\n\n## Certificate trust in the apps and CLIs (v1.1.0)\n\nHow the apps and CLIs check and trust certificates (system trust store first, then trust on first\nuse, changed certificates blocked) is in [certificates](CERTIFICATES.md).\n",
  "agent": "# itcmdr-agent (Rust)\n\nA Windows service (`itcmdr-agent`, LocalSystem) on every PC **and on the office server**. Since v0.20.1 it runs\nthe plugins and schedules, takes its configuration over the link and replaces the nightly task. In v0.17.0 it\nenrolls with the site server and keeps a heartbeat link; the PowerShell agent keeps running the checks\nand plugins from its scheduled tasks, unchanged. Running plugins, schedules and the offline queue move\ninto this agent in later point releases (PLAN.md).\n\nPlaceholders only: SERVER01, WS-01.\n\n## Enroll\n\n1. On the server (admin): `itcmdr-cli agent enroll-token --machine WS-01` (or the app: Agents >\n   Enrollment token). The token is one-time, valid 24 h by default (`--ttl-hours`, max 720) and holds:\n   the address agents dial (default: the server's name and the agent link port, `--address host:port`\n   to override), the SHA-256 of the server's TLS certificate, and a secret (only its hash is stored).\n   The token is never written to the audit log.\n2. On the PC: `itcmdr-agent-Setup-<ver>.exe /VERYSILENT /EnrollToken=<token>` (the installer stores it\n   for Administrators and SYSTEM only; the service enrolls with it on start and deletes it), or by hand:\n   `itcmdr-agent enroll <token> [--machine WS-01] [--role server]`. Use `--role server` for the agent on\n   the office server (its non-responsive alert uses `agent.serverNonresponsiveLevel`).\n3. The agent creates two keys that never leave the PC (`%ProgramData%\\itcmdr\\agent\\keys\\`, DPAPI\n   LocalMachine): an Ed25519 key that signs every Hello, and a TLS client key. It sends the public key and\n   a CSR; the server burns the token and returns a client certificate from the **site CA**\n   (CN = agent id, SAN = machine, 90 days, client auth only). Enrolling a machine again replaces (revokes)\n   its previous enrollment.\n\n## The link\n\n- Port `agent_bind_addr` in server.json (default `0.0.0.0:50052`; `\"\"` turns it off). The server\n  installer opens it in the firewall for the same subnet as 50051.\n- TLS 1.3. The agent **pins** the server certificate's SHA-256 from the token; any other certificate is\n  refused before anything is sent (\"does not match the pinned\"). No public CA is involved.\n- **mTLS**: the server verifies the client certificate against the site CA during the handshake and\n  then requires it to be the exact certificate issued to that agent, not revoked and not expired.\n- The first message is a `Hello` signed with the agent's Ed25519 key over the agent id, machine, time,\n  a nonce and the fingerprint of the client certificate on this connection (clock skew within\n  `auth.max_skew_secs`; nonces are not reusable).\n- Then the server sends `Ping` every `agent.heartbeatSecs` and the agent answers `Pong`. Reconnects use\n  full-jitter exponential backoff, 1 s doubling to `agent.reconnectMaxSecs`.\n\n## Heartbeat states and alerts\n\n`enrolled -> online -> late -> non_responsive`, plus `maintenance` and `revoked`; changes are in the\naudit log (`agent-state`). Missed beats are counted from the last Pong/Hello:\n\n| Setting (per machine overridable) | Default | |\n|---|---|---|\n| `agent.heartbeatSecs` | 60 | ping interval |\n| `agent.lateHeartbeats` | 2 | late, no alert |\n| `agent.missedHeartbeats` | 5 | non-responsive: alert `agent-nonresponsive:<machine>` |\n| `agent.nonresponsiveLevel` / `agent.serverNonresponsiveLevel` | critical | its level |\n| `agent.reconnectMaxSecs` | 300 | backoff cap |\n\nComing back resolves the alert and leaves a closed Informational \"back online\" note. A Pong with the\nclock off by more than 5 minutes raises a Cautious `agent-clock:<machine>` alert.\n\n**Office hours (v0.17.1).** `agent.officeHours` (per machine, e.g. `Mon-Fri 07:00-18:00`, server local\ntime; empty = always) is for PCs that are switched off at night. Outside the hours a missing agent goes\nno further than `late` and raises no alert; inside them, missed beats count from the later of the last\nbeat and the start of today's hours, so a PC switched on at 07:10 is not reported as lost.\n`itcmdr-cli config set agent.officeHours \"Mon-Fri 07:00-18:00\" --machine WS-01`.\n\n## Certificates (v0.17.1)\n\n- **Site CA certified.** The server root key (itcmdr-cli init: itself certified by the master key, docs/SIGNING.md) signs\n  the site CA certificate once (`keys/agent-ca.sig`). Enrollment tokens carry the root public key; at\n  enrollment the agent checks that the server sent that key and a valid signature over the CA, so a\n  token only works with the server that made it. Tokens from a v0.17.0 server (no root) still work.\n  (The server has used the token by then: an enrollment the agent refused shows as `enrolled`, never\n  seen, in `agent list`; revoke it.)\n- **Lifetime and renewal.** Client certificates live `agent.certLifetimeMins` (server setting, default\n  129600 = 90 days). At 2/3 of its life the agent sends a new CSR over the link (new TLS key; the\n  Ed25519 key stays), saves the certificate and reconnects. The replaced certificate keeps working for\n  24 h. `itcmdr-cli agent renew WS-01` asks the agent to renew at its next heartbeat.\n- **Expiry alerts.** `agent-cert:<machine>`: Cautious under 14 days left, Critical under 3 days;\n  resolved by the renewal.\n- **Revocation in the handshake.** The link's TLS layer only accepts client certificates whose serial is\n  the current one of a non-revoked agent (or the one just replaced, during the 24 h overlap). A revoked\n  or superseded certificate fails the TLS handshake itself (logged as `agent link handshake refused`);\n  the agent reports it as refused and stops retrying until it is enrolled again (on Windows the TLS alert\n  often arrives as a plain connection reset, so the agent keeps retrying with backoff; every attempt is\n  refused). The set is reloaded on\n  every heartbeat pass and right after an enroll, renewal or revoke.\n\n## Offline queue (v0.17.1)\n\n`itcmdr-agent alert <key> <title> [--level critical|cautious|informational] [--detail ..] [--resolve]`\n(any local script, run as an administrator) drops the alert into `spool\\` in the agent's data folder.\nThe service moves it into `queue\\` with a durable sequence number (also while the server is unreachable), sends it when linked and deletes it\nwhen the server acks it; the server raises `agent:<machine>:<key>` (or resolves it with `--resolve`) and\nignores a sequence number it has already seen, so a reconnect never duplicates an alert. Alerts queued\nwhile the server was down are delivered after the reconnect, with the time they were queued. The queue\nis capped at `agent.queueMaxMib` (default 256 MiB, sent by the server on connect): the oldest\ninformational items go first, then the oldest of any level, and the drop is reported as a Cautious\n`agent:<machine>:queue-dropped` alert.\n\n## Admin\n\n- `itcmdr-cli agent list [--all]`, `agent revoke WS-01`, `agent renew WS-01` (v0.17.1, CLI only),\n  `agent maintenance WS-01 on|off`; the same (except renew) in the\n  app's Agents page (`ListAgents`, `CreateEnrollToken`, `RevokeAgent`, `SetAgentMaintenance`, admin only,\n  audited).\n- Revoking closes an open link at once (from the app) or at the next heartbeat (from `itcmdr-cli`); the\n  agent is refused from then on and must be enrolled again with a new token.\n- Files on the server: `keys/agent-ca.pem|.key` (site CA), `keys/agent-server.pem|.key` (the pinned\n  server certificate). Keys are secret files (DPAPI / 0600). Replacing `agent-server.*` changes the pin:\n  every agent then needs a new token.\n- The agent's log: `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr\\agent\\logs\\agent.log` (rotated at 10 MiB).\n\n## Plugins and schedules (v0.18.0)\n\n- `agent.plugins` (per machine; `all`, `none` or `name1,name2`) selects the installed, enabled plugins pushed to the agent. Each push carries the files and a root-key endorsement of `itcmdr-agent-plugin/v1\\n<name>\\n<version>\\n<package sha256>`; the agent verifies it, the name/version and the checksums of every file, then swaps the plugin in under `<state>/plugins/<name>`.\n- Enabled schedules with plugins (and the builtin nightly) are pushed as a set with a version; the agent keeps them in `schedules.json` and fires them on its own (time-zone aware; catch-up runs a missed fire once).\n- `itcmdr-cli agent run <machine> <plugin> [--arg k=v]` runs a plugin now; `agent runs` and `agent plugins` list reports and installed plugins.\n- Runs are report-only (`ITCMDR_REPORT_ONLY=1`). Reports go through the offline queue; artifacts land in `<data>/agent-artifacts/<run>/`, logs in `plugin-logs/<plugin>/agents/<machine>.jsonl`, parity as `agent:<run>`.\n- Alerts: `agent-plugin:<machine>:<name>` (install refused), `agent-run:<machine>:<plugin>` (run failed).\n- Enrollment calls `Prepare` first (CA and root key verified before the token is used); a disconnected agent calls `Check` and, when revoked, stops reconnecting (logs \"enroll it again\", checks every 15 min).\n\n## Config and the switch to the agent (v0.20.1)\nThe server sends each agent a config document over the link (`ConfigUpdate`): the cutover\nstate and the machine's agent settings, signed by the server root key. The agent keeps it in\n`config.json` only when the signature checks, and checks it again whenever it reads it. A broken\nor edited file is deleted: the agent stays report-only, raises a `config-rejected` alert and gets\nthe config again from the server within a minute.\n\n`itcmdr-cli cutover switch --to agent` hands the nightly run to the Rust agent. It is refused\nuntil the normal gate passes (`cutover status`) and every machine active in the last two days has\na Rust agent that is enrolled, not revoked, seen in the last 10 minutes, holds the current config\nand has clean parity. After the switch the agent runs plugins for real and keeps the PowerShell\n`itcmdr Agent Nightly` task disabled (it disables it again and alerts if someone turns it back\non; the PowerShell poller does the same through `itcmdr-agent alert`, so the alert is raised\nwhichever of them notices first). A missing or rejected `config.json` leaves the task as it is;\nonly a signed config from the server hands it back. There is no PowerShell fallback. Running the\nswitch again once it is done changes nothing. `itcmdr-cli cutover rollback` undoes it: the agents\nre-enable the task, resolve any `nightly-task-reenabled` alert and go back to report-only.\n",
  "alerts": "# Alerts\n\nThe site server raises alerts; there are no outside notifiers. They show in the site app (Alerts\npage and badge) and in `itcmdr-cli alerts`, and, when the site is linked, go up to\n[adminsvr](ADMINSVR.md) redacted (opaque ids, codes from a fixed vocabulary).\n\n- **Levels:** `critical`, `cautious`, `informational`.\n- **Sources:** `plugin` (plugin errors), `check` (agent check results), `backup` (backup outcome),\n  `package` (quarantined packages), `server` (for example agent heartbeats, certificate expiry, upgrades).\n- **Dedupe:** one row per key while it is open, acknowledged or silenced (the count goes up and the\n  level only goes up). A resolved alert raised again within `alerts.dedupe_window_mins` reopens.\n- **Auto-resolve:** the same source reporting ok resolves it.\n- **Silences:** by machine and/or source for some hours; new matching alerts are stored as silenced.\n\n## Commands\n\n    itcmdr-cli alerts list [--state open|resolved|all|acked|silenced] [--level critical] [--machine WS-01] [--source backup]\n    itcmdr-cli alerts ack <id>\n    itcmdr-cli alerts resolve <id>\n    itcmdr-cli alerts silence --machine WS-01 --hours 4 --reason \"moving desks\"\n\nAn agent (or any local admin script) can queue an alert that is delivered also after an outage:\n`itcmdr-agent alert <key> <title> [--level ...] [--detail ...] [--resolve]` ([agent](AGENT.md)).\n\nadminsvr has its own alerts for the sites it watches (`itcmdr-admin alert list|ack|resolve`, push\nnotifications) and for itself (for example `tls-acme`).\n\n- `installer-server` / `installer-agent` (v1.2.1, Critical): the last Windows installer run of that package failed; the title names the step and the version still installed, the detail has the exit code and the log path. A good run resolves it. See docs/UPGRADES.md, Installer logs.\n",
  "architecture": "# itcmdr architecture\n\nSite-specific values live only in gitignored files (`config/site.json`, `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\site.json`, `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\server.json`). Everything in this repo uses placeholders: RFC 5737 ranges (192.0.2.x, 198.51.100.x, 203.0.113.x) and names like `SERVER01`, `WS-01`, `RCU-01`, `PRN-01`.\n\n## Components\n\n| Part | Where it runs | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Agent (`agent/Invoke-ItcmdrAgent.ps1`) | every PC, nightly as SYSTEM | checks network config, connectivity and services, repairs what it safely can, writes one JSON Lines log per run |\n| Poller (`agent/Poll-ItcmdrRequests.ps1`) | every PC, every minute as SYSTEM | runs on-demand requests from the app |\n| Rotation (`agent/Rotate-ItcmdrLogs.ps1`) | server, daily | zips logs weekly, into 4-week blocks, and yearly |\n| Server (`itcmdr-server`, Rust/tonic, SQLite) | server, Windows service | ingests logs, computes metrics, serves gRPC and gRPC-Web on port 50051 |\n| App (Flutter) | Windows desktop or web | Overview, Metrics, Logs, Exceptions, On-demand, Schedules, Plugins, Backup, Audit, Keys, Settings |\n\nThe agent and poller run under Windows PowerShell 5.1 (the `powershell.exe` used by the scheduled tasks) and under PowerShell 7.\n\n## Agent runs and logs\n\n- Each run writes `<paths.localLogs>\\<COMPUTER>_<yyyyMMdd-HHmmss>_<runId>.log` (site.json; default `C:\\maintenanceRuns`): a `run-start` record, one `check` record per check (`checkId`, `category`, `status`, `attemptedRepair`, `result`, `ts`, `excepted`, ...), then `run-complete`.\n- The log is copied to `shares.maintenanceRuns`. If the share is unreachable, the log is queued in `<paths.agentData>\\spool\\outbox` (default `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr`) and sent on the next run.\n- Repairs run only as SYSTEM, or with `-Repair`, and never with `-ReportOnly`.\n- The clinical guard: if files under `services.rcu.dataPath` changed in the last 10 minutes, the run is report-only unless `-Force` is given.\n- Checks that have no safe automatic repair (port and share reachability) are logged as failures without a repair attempt.\n- ExampleVendor configuration is never edited by the agent.\n- `-Categories network,connectivity,services` limits a run to those categories.\n\n## Exceptions (allowlist)\n\nOne JSON shape is used everywhere:\n\n```json\n{\"id\":\"\u2026\",\"scope\":{\"machines\":[\"WS-01\"],\"role\":\"Workstation\"},\"checkId\":\"NET-SERVER-TCP-445\",\n \"checkPattern\":\"^PRN-\",\"action\":\"ignore|downgrade:warn|downgrade:info\",\"reason\":\"\u2026\",\"expires\":\"2099-12-31\"}\n```\n\n- A rule with no machines and no role applies to every machine.\n- `expires` given as a date is valid through that whole day.\n- The agent combines the `exceptions` in `site.json` with the server-managed `<maintenanceRuns share>\\exceptions.json`. It caches the latter in `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr` for offline runs.\n- The server rewrites `exceptions.json` on every add, edit or expire, and at startup.\n\n## On-demand runs\n\n1. The app calls `TriggerRuns`, which requires the token. The server records the request, writes `<logs_dir>\\requests\\<id>.json` and adds an audit entry.\n2. Each poller picks up requests addressed to its computer, or to all computers (an empty `machines` list). It takes a per-machine lock and writes `responses\\<id>.<COMPUTER>.json` with `running`.\n3. The poller runs the agent with `-ReportOnly`, `-Repair`, `-Force` and `-Categories` taken from the request, then rewrites the response as `done` or `failed`.\n4. The server folds the responses into the request status, which is one of:\n   - `queued`: no responses yet\n   - `running`: a response is still running, or a listed machine hasn't answered\n   - `done`: every response is done\n   - `failed`: every response finished and at least one failed\n\n   Status is updated on every ingest pass and on each `GetRequestStatus`.\n\nNo WinRM or remote PowerShell is needed.\n\n## Server\n\n- **Ingest** runs at startup and then every `ingest_interval_secs` (default 30).\n  - It reads `.log`/`.jsonl` files and zips under `logs_dir`, recursively, including zips nested in zips. Archive names don't matter.\n  - Files whose size and mtime are unchanged are skipped.\n  - Runs are unique by `runId` and checks by `(runId, checkId)`, so re-reading logs from archives never double-counts.\n  - Timestamps come from the records (`started`, `ts`, `finished`).\n  - Events are applied in time order.\n- **Incidents** are tracked per (machine, check):\n  - A failure opens an incident, unless one is already open.\n  - The next `ok` closes it.\n  - A check that was broken and repaired in the same run counts as an incident with zero repair time.\n  - Excepted checks never open incidents.\n- **Metrics** (`GetMetrics`, windows of 7, 30, 90 or 365 days):\n  - Per bucket: `incidents:<m>`, `uptime:<m>` (share of days with a run), `repair_failures_bucket:<m>`.\n  - Over the window: `incidents_total:<m>` (the ranking), `open_incidents:<m>`, `mttr:<m>` (ms), `repair_failures:<m>:<check>`, `missed_runs:<m>`.\n- **Rollups** are aligned to ISO weeks:\n  - `weekly`: an ISO week, Monday to Sunday.\n  - `block4`: block N of an ISO year covers ISO weeks 4N-3 to 4N. There are 13 blocks per year, and in 53-week years week 53 belongs to block 13.\n  - `yearly`: an ISO year.\n  - `daily` is also available. Calendar months and quarters are not used.\n- **Auth**: calls carry an Ed25519 signature (`x-itcmdr-key`, `x-itcmdr-ts`, `x-itcmdr-nonce`, `x-itcmdr-sig`) over the canonical request bytes. See `docs/SIGNING.md`. The server checks the registered public key, a clock window and a nonce cache, then the role (owner > admin > user > client).\n  - New installs default to `auth_mode = signed` (a key is required, including for reads). An install from before v0.12.0 with no value in server.json keeps `both`: signed calls, or the legacy token for changes. `itcmdr-cli auth status` shows which. `auth set-mode signed` retires the token.\n  - The legacy token is `authorization: Bearer <token>`. The server compares its SHA-256 with `admin_token_hash`. Restore and key registration never accept the token.\n  - If no hash is configured and the mode still allows the token, changes are refused with an explanation, and a warning is logged at startup.\n  - `allow_insecure_no_auth: true` is the explicit lab-only opt-out.\n  - Restrict the listen port with the firewall rule.\n- **Audit log**: on-demand triggers and exception add, edit and expire are recorded with actor, time and detail. The actor is the app's `x-itcmdr-actor` name. Read it with `ListAudit`.\n- **Live updates**: `WatchUpdates` streams events (`run`, `check`, `incident-open`, `incident-close`, `request`, `exception-*`, `audit`). The app uses them to refresh.\n- **Retention**: runs, checks, closed incidents, rollups and finished requests older than `retention_days` (default 400) are deleted at startup and daily. The audit log and exceptions are kept.\n\n## Log rotation (`Rotate-ItcmdrLogs.ps1`, daily on the server)\n\nAll periods are ISO-week aligned. The current week is never touched, and a zip is built only after its whole period has ended.\n\n```\n<maintenanceRuns>\\archive\\weekly\\itcmdr-<isoYear>-W<ww>.zip   logs of one finished ISO week\n<maintenanceRuns>\\archive\\blocks\\itcmdr-<isoYear>-B<NN>.zip   the 4 (block 13 in a 53-week year: 5) weekly zips\n<maintenanceRuns>\\archive\\yearly\\itcmdr-<isoYear>.zip          all block zips of the ISO year\n```\n\n- **Manifest:** every zip contains `manifest.json` with the kind, ISO year and week or block, the `start`/`end` dates, and the SHA256 of every member. The zip is re-opened and verified before the originals are deleted.\n- **Late logs:** logs that arrive for a period that's already zipped go into a numbered supplement, such as `itcmdr-2026-W43-2.zip`.\n- **Block zips after yearly:** they're kept unless `retention.keepBlockZipsAfterYearly` is `false`. The legacy `yearlyKeepForever` is honoured.\n- **Backup:** the whole `maintenanceRuns` folder, including `archive\\`, is what the nightly backup must cover (`backup.expectedBackupSources`).\n\n## Regenerating gRPC code\n\n- Rust: `server/build.rs` regenerates `server/src/generated/itcmdr.rs` with a vendored protoc on every build.\n- Dart: `protoc -I proto --dart_out=grpc:app/lib/proto proto/itcmdr.proto` using protoc_plugin 25.1.0 (`dart pub global activate protoc_plugin 25.1.0`), matching protobuf ^6.1 and grpc ^5.2 in `app/pubspec.lock`.\n\n## Demo data\n\n`cargo run -p itcmdr-sample-data-gen -- --out-dir ./sample-maintenanceRuns --days 90` writes agent-format logs for placeholder machines, with varying failure rates, failed repairs and missed nights. Point `logs_dir` at that folder (see `config/server.example.json`).\n",
  "backup-adminsvr": "# adminsvr backup and restore (v1.2.0)\n\nadminsvr backs up **its own** state so a lost or broken adminsvr machine can be rebuilt with the\nsame identity: enrolled devices and linked sites keep working without re-enrolling or re-linking.\n\n## What is in an archive, and what never is\n\nAn archive (`itcmdr-adminsvr-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSSmmm-<kind>.itcab`) holds exactly these files:\n\n| File | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `db/adminsvr.sqlite` or `db/postgres.json` | consistent snapshot of adminsvr's database (SQLite `VACUUM INTO`; Postgres: every table as JSON) |\n| `keys/root.key` | adminsvr's root key (signs commands and user endorsements; sites pin it) |\n| `tls/ca.pem`, `tls/ca.key`, `tls/server.pem`, `tls/server.key` | the CA for site client certificates and the internal server certificate (devices pin it) |\n| `acme/**` | the ACME (Let's Encrypt) account and certificate, when used |\n| `backup/recovery.json` | the recovery public keys (below) |\n| `config/adminsvr.env` | the `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_*` settings in effect (from the env file and environment; flags given on a command line are not included) |\n| `push/*` | the FCM service account and APNs key files, when configured |\n\nAnything else in an archive is refused when it is opened. **Site backups are never included,\nfetched, decrypted or inspected**: they stay on each site (see [Backup and restore](BACKUP.md)).\nNothing from offices is in an archive beyond what adminsvr's database already holds (the redacted\nstatus, metrics and alerts the sites send).\n\n## Encryption\n\nLike the site server's backups: each archive is encrypted (XChaCha20-Poly1305) with a fresh\nrandom key, and that key is sealed for a **recovery key** rebuilt from 24 recovery words. adminsvr\nstores only the recovery public keys, so it can make backups but cannot open them. The header\n(versions, schema version, the size and sha256 of every file) is signed by adminsvr's root key, and\n`backup init` certifies the root key with the recovery key, so a restore checks the whole chain.\n\nThese are adminsvr's own words, separate from any site's 24 words. Keep them offline, apart from\nthe backups (an archive and its words together are a full copy of adminsvr's identity).\n\n## Set up\n\n```\nitcmdr-adminsvr backup init            # prints 24 recovery words ONCE; write them down\nitcmdr-adminsvr backup status          # recovery key, folder, schedule, newest backup, last drill\n```\n\n`backup init --replace` issues new words (for example after the root key changed); archives made\nbefore still need the old words.\n\nSettings (flags, `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_*` in the environment or the env file):\n\n| Setting | Default | |\n|---|---|---|\n| `--backup-dir` / `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_BACKUP_DIR` | `<data dir>/backups` | point it at another disk or a mounted share |\n| `--backup-hour` / `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_BACKUP_HOUR` | `3` | nightly at this local hour while `serve` runs; `off` to schedule it yourself |\n| `--backup-keep` / `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_BACKUP_KEEP` | `14` | nightly archives kept; manual ones are never pruned |\n\nNothing runs until `backup init` has been done. To use your own scheduler instead (cron, a systemd\ntimer, Task Scheduler), set `--backup-hour off` and run `itcmdr-adminsvr backup create` and\n`backup prune` with the same settings as the service.\n\n## Commands (on the adminsvr machine, as the service user)\n\n```\nitcmdr-adminsvr backup create [--out DIR]\nitcmdr-adminsvr backup list [--dir DIR]\nitcmdr-adminsvr backup verify ARCHIVE           # signature, hash, this adminsvr's recovery key\nitcmdr-adminsvr backup verify --deep ARCHIVE    # words on stdin: also decrypt and check every file\nitcmdr-adminsvr backup drill [--deep]           # restore drill (below)\nitcmdr-adminsvr backup prune\nitcmdr-adminsvr restore --from ARCHIVE [--dry-run]   # words on stdin; stop the service first\n```\n\nThe words are read from stdin only (typed, or piped from a file you delete afterwards), never\nfrom a flag, and never logged.\n\n## Restore drill\n\nThe drill proves the newest archive can be restored without touching the live install: it copies\nthe archive to a temp folder, checks the signature chain, the hash, that it was made for this\nadminsvr's recovery key and that its schema is not newer, then deletes the copy. `--deep` (words\non stdin) also decrypts it, checks every file, checks the root key and certificates, and restores\nthe database into a throwaway copy (integrity check and migrations). The result is kept in\n`<data dir>/backup/last-drill.json` (`backup status` shows it) and audited (`backup.drill`, the\narchive name and ok/failed only).\n\nRunbook: monthly `backup drill`; quarterly (and after any key or setting change) `backup drill\n--deep` with the words typed in; once a year a full restore onto a spare machine or VM.\n\n## Restore\n\n1. Install the same or a newer adminsvr. Stop the `itcmdr-adminsvr` service (restore refuses\n   while something answers on the admin API port).\n2. `itcmdr-adminsvr restore --from <archive> --dry-run`, type the words: everything is opened and\n   checked, and the files it would write are listed. Nothing changes.\n3. The same without `--dry-run`. It:\n   - refuses wrong words, a damaged or truncated archive, an archive for another recovery key\n     (on an install that already has one), a newer schema, and a database type that does not\n     match `--db-url` (SQLite archive into SQLite, Postgres into Postgres);\n   - copies the current state to `<data dir>/pre-restore-<ts>/`;\n   - writes the root key, TLS files, ACME files, recovery key and the database, then migrates an\n     older schema;\n   - puts the archived settings and push keys in `<data dir>/restored/` for review: the active\n     configuration is never overwritten (paths may differ on the new machine);\n   - puts the safety copy back if any step fails.\n4. Review `restored/config/adminsvr.env`, then start the service. Devices and sites reconnect with\n   the same certificate and root key.\n\nThe audit chain is restored with the database; the restore itself is audited (`backup.restore`).\n",
  "backup": "# Backup and restore\n\nThis page is the site server's backup. adminsvr's own backup (v1.2.0) is in\n[adminsvr backup and restore](BACKUP-ADMINSVR.md); adminsvr never holds or opens site backups.\n\nWhat's in an archive (`itcmdr-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSSmmm-<kind>.itcb`):\n\n| File | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `itcmdr.database` | consistent snapshot of the live SQLCipher DB (still encrypted with db.key) |\n| `keys/db.key.escrow` | db.key sealed for the master key |\n| `server.json` | the config in effect |\n| `service_plugins/**` | installed plugin packages (v0.9.0) |\n| `quarantine/index.jsonl` | refused packages (the quarantined files themselves are not archived) |\n| `plugin-logs/**` | per-run plugin logs, `itcmdr.log/v1` (v0.10.0); a restore merges them and keeps existing files |\n\nPlugin config lives in the database (settings). `keys/db.key` and `keys/root` are never archived: they are bound to the machine.\n\nThe whole archive is encrypted for the master key (only the 24 words open it). The header (manifest\nwith versions, schema version and sha256 per file) is signed by the server's root key, which carries a\nmaster certificate. The signature covers the ciphertext hash.\n\n## Nightly\n\nSettings can also be changed at runtime (stored in the DB, they win over server.json):\n`itcmdr-cli config get`, `itcmdr-cli config set backup.dir <folder>` (must exist and be writable),\n`config unset <key>`, or gRPC `GetSettings` / `UpdateSettings`.\n\n\nserver.json `backup`: `dir` (default `<data_dir>/backups`; point it at the share), `nightly` (true),\n`hour` (2, local), `keep_daily` / `keep_weekly` / `keep_blocks` (7 / 4 / 13; a block is ISO weeks 4N-3..4N, 13 per year, week 53 in block 13; the old name `keep_monthly` is still read). Only `nightly`\narchives are pruned. Nothing runs until `init` has been done.\n\n## Commands (run on the server, as an admin)\n\n```\nitcmdr-cli backup create [--out DIR]\nitcmdr-cli backup list [--dir DIR]\nitcmdr-cli backup verify ARCHIVE            # signature + hash, pinned to this server's master key\nitcmdr-cli backup verify --deep ARCHIVE     # words on stdin: decrypt, check every file\nitcmdr-cli backup prune [--dir DIR]\nitcmdr-cli restore --from ARCHIVE [--dry-run]   # words on stdin; stop the service first\n```\n\ngRPC: `CreateBackup`, `ListBackups`, `VerifyBackup` (admin or owner, audit-logged).\n\n## Restore drill (v0.22.0)\n\nThe drill proves the newest backup can be restored without touching the live server. Everything\nhappens on the site; adminsvr can only ask for it and see ok/failed.\n\n```\nitcmdr-cli backup drill           # copy the newest archive to a temp folder, check it, delete the copy\nitcmdr-cli backup drill --deep    # words on stdin: also decrypt it and check every file\n```\n\nThe quick drill checks the signature chain, that the archive was made by this server (master\nkey), the contents hash, the manifest and that its schema is not newer than this itcmdr. `--deep`\nadds decryption and every file's size and sha256. The result is audit-logged (`backup.drill`) and\nkept as the last drill result; the archive and the words never leave the machine.\n\nFrom adminsvr (once the site owner allows `backup.drill`; new links and sites on the v0.20/v0.21\ndefault list get it automatically):\n\n```\nitcmdr-admin backup drill <site>   # waits for the site's ok/failed\n```\n\nThe admin app has a Restore drill button per server (Servers page).\n\n### Deep-check reminder (v1.0.0)\n\nThe server keeps the date and result (ok or failed) of the last `backup drill --deep`. The site app\nshows \"Last deep check: N days ago\" on the Backup page, with a warning when it never ran, failed or\nis older than 90 days. A site linked to adminsvr sends only that date and result (no backup\ncontents, names or paths) in its status; adminsvr's CLI (`itcmdr-admin site list`) and the admin\napp (Servers) show it and warn on the same rules, with the limit set per organization\n(`itcmdr-admin org policy --deep-check-days N`, owners only).\n\n### Runbook: monthly restore test\n\n1. Monthly, from adminsvr or on the server: `backup drill`. A failure is a Critical problem:\n   take a fresh backup (`backup create`) and drill again; if it still fails, check the backup\n   folder (disk, permissions) and the server log.\n2. Quarterly (or after any key or backup setting change), on the server, in person:\n   `itcmdr-cli backup drill --deep` with the 24 words typed in. Never paste the words anywhere else.\n3. Once a year, a full restore onto a spare machine or VM (\"Restore onto a replacement machine\"\n   below) with the newest archive; check the app signs in and the machines and history are there,\n   then wipe the spare.\n4. Write the date and result in the site notes.\n\n## Restore from the app (v0.10.0, server side)\n\n`StageRestore {backup, words}` (owner only, audited as `restore-stage`; the words are never logged):\n\n1. The archive (a name from `ListBackups`) is fully verified and decrypted with the words; nothing\n   changes if that fails.\n2. `restore/job.json` is written and `itcmdr-restore` is started (detached on Windows). The words\n   go to it on stdin only.\n3. The helper runs `restore.stop_command` (server.json, default `sc.exe stop itcmdr-server`), takes\n   the `pre-restore-<ts>` safety copy, restores, runs `restore.start_command` and health-checks:\n   the DB opens with the right schema, `integrity_check` passes and gRPC answers, within\n   `restore.health_timeout_secs`.\n4. If any step after the copy fails, the safety copy is put back and the service restarted\n   (`rolled-back`, audited `restore-rolled-back`); otherwise `done` (`restore-done`).\n\n`GetRestoreStatus` (admin) returns the state (`staged`, `stopping`, `restoring`, `starting`,\n`health-check`, `rolling-back`, `done`, `rolled-back`, `failed`) and every step from\n`restore/status.json`. The live server.json is never overwritten.\n\n## Restore onto a replacement machine\n\n1. Install itcmdr, stop the `itcmdr-server` service.\n2. `itcmdr-cli restore --from \\\\SERVER01\\share\\itcmdr\\backups\\<archive> --dry-run`, type the words.\n3. Same without `--dry-run`. It:\n   - copies any existing state to `<data_dir>/pre-restore-<ts>/`,\n   - writes the database and escrow, rebuilds `keys/db.key` from the escrow (DPAPI),\n   - refuses an archive from a newer schema; an older one is migrated when the DB opens,\n   - issues a new root key (the old machine's root key is revoked),\n   - writes the archived config to `<data_dir>/restored-server.json` for review (the active\n     server.json is left alone; paths may differ on the new machine).\n4. Start the service.\n",
  "certificates": "# HTTPS and certificate trust\n\nSince v1.1.0 every API is HTTPS by default: the site server's API, adminsvr's admin API, and the\nSiteLink and agent link (pinned, with client certificates). This page covers how the apps and\nCLIs decide whether to trust a server.\n\n## Which certificate each server has\n\n| Server | Certificate | Pinned by |\n|---|---|---|\n| site server API (50051) and agent link (50052) | `<data dir>\\keys\\agent-server.pem`, from the site CA | agents (exact pin); the site app and `itcmdr-cli` via trust on first use |\n| adminsvr admin API (8444) | internal certificate `<data dir>/tls/server.pem`, plus an optional Let's Encrypt certificate ([deploying adminsvr](DEPLOY-ADMINSVR.md)) | enrolled devices (pin from the enrollment code); browsers and new clients via the system store |\n| adminsvr SiteLink (8443) | internal certificate only, mTLS | sites (pin from the site token) |\n\n## Trust in the apps and CLIs\n\nThe admin app, the site app, `itcmdr-admin` and `itcmdr-cli` use HTTPS by default and validate\nthe server against the system trust store (chain, name, expiry). When that fails they do not just\nfail and never ignore errors silently: they show the certificate's **SHA-256 fingerprint,\nsubject and expiry** and offer **Trust this certificate**, which trusts exactly that certificate for\nthat one server (trust on first use). Compare the fingerprint with the server first\n(`itcmdr-adminsvr tls-status`, or the server's `agent-server.pem`).\n- A **different** certificate than the trusted one is refused with a loud warning until the user\n  reviews it and replaces the trusted one (app: tick \"I checked the new fingerprint\", then\n  **Replace trusted certificate**; CLI: `itcmdr-admin trust review`, then\n  `itcmdr-admin trust set <sha256> --replace`).\n- An **expired / not yet valid / wrong-name** certificate needs an explicit override on top\n  (\"Trust anyway\" with a confirmation tick; CLI `--trust-anyway`).\n- Trusted certificates can be listed and removed: site app Settings \u2192 Trusted certificates;\n  admin app Settings \u2192 Certificate; `itcmdr-admin trust show | review | remove`.\n- `itcmdr-admin --trust-fingerprint <sha256>` trusts the certificate non-interactively (it must be\n  the one the server presents); on a terminal it asks instead (type `trust`, or `trust anyway`).\n- A certificate the system trust store accepts for the right name is accepted even when an older\n  certificate is trusted (for example a self-signed one trusted before the server got a Let's\n  Encrypt certificate); the trusted one is kept as the fallback for when the system store refuses.\n  A changed certificate that the system store does not accept is always blocked.\n- **HTTPS and plain HTTP are both supported; HTTPS is the default.** Plain HTTP is an explicit\n  opt-in: the site app's \"Use HTTPS\" switch off, or `itcmdr-cli --plain-http`. The site server\n  always serves HTTPS, accepts plain HTTP from the same machine, and from other machines only with\n  `api_plain_http: true` in server.json (default false; `itcmdr-cli setup apply --api-plain-http\n  true`), for example behind a TLS-terminating proxy.\n- **v1.0 apps and a v1.1 server:** v1.0 site apps talk plain HTTP. Until they are updated, set\n  `api_plain_http: true` on the server (then back to false after the update).\n- Enrolled adminsvr devices keep the pin from their enrollment code (the internal certificate),\n  and sites and agents keep their exact pins and mTLS; none of that depends on the system trust store.\n\n## Extra roots\n\n`ITCMDR_EXTRA_ROOTS` adds PEM root certificates (a list of files, separated like `PATH`) to the\nsystem trust store for the CLIs, for example a private CA.\n",
  "cli": "# itcmdr-cli: site server administration\n\n`itcmdr-cli` runs on the site server (it comes with the server package; the CLI package puts it on\nPATH on other machines). Most commands read the server's data folder directly; some talk to the\nrunning server over HTTPS, pinned to the local server certificate (`--plain-http` is the explicit\nopt-in for plain HTTP). Commands that need the 24 words ask for them on the console and never log them.\n\nGlobal options: `--data-dir <dir>` (beats `ITCMDR_DATA_DIR` and server.json), `--json`, `--plain-http`.\nThe full list of commands and flags is in `man itcmdr-cli` ([man pages](MAN.md)).\n\n| Command | What |\n|---|---|\n| `setup check \\| apply \\| first-run` | install checks, site values into server.json, first-run (24 words, first owner key) |\n| `init`, `recover-db-key` | create the master key; restore `keys\\db.key` on a replacement machine |\n| `keys certify \\| new \\| register \\| list \\| revoke \\| rotate` | [keys](KEYS.md) |\n| `user add` | certify and register a person's key |\n| `auth status \\| set-mode` | signed-only or the legacy token ([install](INSTALL.md)) |\n| `config list \\| get \\| set \\| unset` | runtime [settings](SETTINGS.md) |\n| `schedule ...`, `cutover ...` | [schedules](SCHEDULES.md) |\n| `plugins ...`, `service register` | [plugins](PLUGINS.md) |\n| `agent enroll-token \\| list \\| revoke \\| renew \\| run \\| runs \\| plugins \\| maintenance` | [agents](AGENT.md) |\n| `alerts list \\| ack \\| resolve \\| silence` | [alerts](ALERTS.md) |\n| `backup ...`, `restore`, `restore-stage`, `restore-status` | [backup and restore](BACKUP.md) |\n| `upgrade ...` | agent rollouts and server upgrades ([upgrades](UPGRADES.md)) |\n| `calls list \\| show \\| prune` | the call log |\n| `adminsvr enroll \\| status \\| unlink \\| share-names \\| allow` | the offsite link ([adminsvr](ADMINSVR.md)) |\n| `help [topic]` | these guides in the terminal ([in-app help](HELP.md)) |\n\nExit status: 0 on success, 1 on an error (message on stderr; `setup check` exits 1 when a check\nfails), 2 for invalid usage.\n",
  "deploy-adminsvr": "# Deploying adminsvr with Let's Encrypt\n\nadminsvr is optional: it lets an IT provider watch many sites. It only ever sees redacted status and\nnever holds backups. The domain is set per install; `admin.example.com` below is a placeholder.\n\n1. **A host** with a public address (Linux with systemd is the simplest; macOS, Windows and a\n   container also work, see [adminsvr](ADMINSVR.md)).\n2. **DNS:** an A/AAAA record for `admin.example.com` pointing at it.\n3. **Firewall:** allow TCP 8444 (admin API) and 8443 (SiteLink). For Let's Encrypt also forward\n   **443 to 8444** (`tls-alpn-01`, the default) or open **80** (`http-01`).\n4. **Install:** `sudo ./install.sh` from `itcmdr-adminsvr-<ver>-linux-x86_64.tar.gz`.\n5. **Settings** in `/etc/itcmdr-adminsvr/adminsvr.env`:\n\n        ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_PUBLIC_API=admin.example.com:8444\n        ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_PUBLIC_SITE=admin.example.com:8443\n        ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_TLS_NAMES=admin.example.com\n        ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_DOMAINS=admin.example.com\n        ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_CONTACT=it@example.com\n        ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_DIRECTORY=staging      # switch to production once staging works\n        # ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_CHALLENGE=http-01    # if you cannot forward 443\n        # ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_DB_URL=postgres://...      # default: SQLite in the data folder\n\n6. **First owner:** `sudo -u itcmdr-adminsvr itcmdr-adminsvr --env-file /etc/itcmdr-adminsvr/adminsvr.env init --email you@example.com --name \"You\"`.\n7. **Start:** `systemctl enable --now itcmdr-adminsvr`; then `itcmdr-adminsvr tls-status` shows\n   `acme`, the names and the expiry. Switch the directory to `production`, restart, check again.\n8. **Enroll your device:** `itcmdr-admin enroll <code>` or the [admin app](ADMIN-APP.md).\n9. **Link sites:** `itcmdr-admin site create <name>`, then on the site server\n   `itcmdr-cli adminsvr enroll <site token>`.\n\nRenewal is automatic (30 days before expiry, no restart). A failed renewal keeps the current\ncertificate and opens the `tls-acme` alert. Enrolled devices and sites keep using the pinned\ninternal certificate, so turning ACME on or off never breaks them. All settings:\n[environment](ENVIRONMENT.md).\n",
  "deploy-site-server": "# Deploying a site server\n\nA checklist for one office. Placeholders: SERVER01 (the server), WS-01 (a PC), 198.51.100.0/24 (the LAN).\nDetails for each step are in [install](INSTALL.md).\n\n1. **Pick the machine.** Windows, always on, on the office LAN. Keep a second disk or NAS folder\n   for backups if you have one (not required: v1.0 needs no file share).\n2. **Install the server** as an administrator, at the machine:\n   `itcmdr-server-Setup-<ver>.exe /AllowSubnet=198.51.100.0/24` (or the wizard). This adds the\n   `itcmdr-server` service, the watchdog, firewall rules for 50051 (API) and 50052 (agent link)\n   limited to the subnet, and `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\server.json`.\n3. **First-run setup, in person:** the 24 words are shown once. Write them on paper and keep it off\n   the server. The first owner's app key is registered.\n4. **Check:** `itcmdr-cli setup check` (exit 0), `Get-Service itcmdr-server` Running, and no errors in\n   `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\server-logs\\itcmdr-server.txt`.\n5. **Backups:** `itcmdr-cli setup apply --backup-dir <second disk>`; `itcmdr-cli backup create`, then\n   `backup drill` (and monthly `backup drill --deep` with the words; [backup](BACKUP.md)).\n6. **Agents:** for each PC, `itcmdr-cli agent enroll-token --machine WS-01`, then\n   `itcmdr-agent-Setup-<ver>.exe /VERYSILENT /EnrollToken=<token>`. `itcmdr-cli agent list` shows it online.\n7. **Apps:** install the site app on the admin PCs; Settings: `SERVER01 : 50051`, HTTPS on; compare and\n   trust the certificate fingerprint ([certificates](CERTIFICATES.md)); certify the key ([keys](KEYS.md)).\n8. **Updates:** set the update window (`itcmdr-cli config set updates.window \"daily 01:00-04:00\"`;\n   [upgrades](UPGRADES.md)).\n9. **Optional offsite link:** `itcmdr-cli adminsvr enroll <site token>` and allow only the commands you\n   want ([adminsvr](ADMINSVR.md)). Nothing internal leaves the office; backups never do.\n\nNever test break scenarios on a production server; use a test machine or VM.\n",
  "deploy-website": "# The website\n\nThe public site (itcmdr.weirdbrains.com) is static HTML on the `site` branch of this repository,\nserved by GitHub Pages (`CNAME`, `.nojekyll`). It has no build dependencies.\n\n- Work in a separate worktree: `git worktree add ../itcmdr-site site`.\n- Pages come from `build.py` (home, download, docs, security): edit it, then `python3 build.py`.\n- Download links point at the release assets; update the version and links after each release.\n- Before pushing, run the leak scans (`python3 scripts/scan_repo.py`) and check every link.\n- Push the branch: `git push origin site`. Pages serves the branch; HTTPS is enforced.\n- DNS: a CNAME for the domain pointing at the GitHub Pages host; the certificate is issued by GitHub.\n- Drafts (posts) live in `drafts/`, untracked, and are never published without review.\n",
  "environment": "# Environment variables\n\nEvery `ITCMDR_*` variable read by itcmdr. The [man pages](MAN.md) are generated from this table, and\na test fails when a variable in the source is missing here. adminsvr flags each have an\n`ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_*` variable; values in the environment win over the env file.\n\n## Programs\n\n| Variable | Used by | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ITCMDR_SERVER_CONFIG` | itcmdr-server, itcmdr-cli | path of server.json |\n| `ITCMDR_DATA_DIR` | itcmdr-server, itcmdr-cli | data folder (`--data-dir` beats it; it beats server.json) |\n| `ITCMDR_SERVER_PROGRAM` | itcmdr-server | the server program folder for self-upgrade (default: the exe's folder) |\n| `ITCMDR_RESTORE_BIN` | itcmdr-server | path of itcmdr-restore (default: next to the server) |\n| `ITCMDR_PLUGIN_PS_HOST` | itcmdr-server, itcmdr-agent | path of itcmdr-plugin-ps (default: next to the exe) |\n| `ITCMDR_POWERSHELL` | itcmdr-plugin-ps, itcmdr-server | PowerShell to run (default powershell.exe on Windows, pwsh elsewhere) |\n| `ITCMDR_PLUGIN_SCRIPT` | itcmdr-plugin-ps | the plugin script, instead of `--script` |\n| `ITCMDR_AGENT_DIR` | itcmdr-agent, itcmdr-agent-watchdog | agent data folder (default %ProgramData%\\itcmdr\\agent, /var/lib/itcmdr-agent) |\n| `ITCMDR_AGENT_PROGRAM` | itcmdr-agent | the agent program folder (holding `current\\`) for self-upgrade |\n| `ITCMDR_PLUGIN_START_SECS` | itcmdr-agent | seconds a plugin has to answer the handshake (default 60) |\n| `ITCMDR_PS_MODULE` | itcmdr-agent, plugins | path of Itcmdr.Plugin.psm1 for PowerShell plugins |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMIN_HOME` | itcmdr-admin | this device's identity folder (default: the user config folder) |\n| `ITCMDR_FIDO2_DEVICE` | itcmdr-admin | libfido2 device path for passkeys (default: the first one) |\n| `ITCMDR_EXTRA_ROOTS` | itcmdr-admin, itcmdr-cli | extra PEM root files added to the system trust store (PATH-style list) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ENV_FILE` | itcmdr-adminsvr | KEY=VALUE settings file |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_DB_URL` | itcmdr-adminsvr | `postgres://...` or `sqlite://<path>` (default SQLite in the data folder) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_DATA_DIR` | itcmdr-adminsvr | data folder (default /var/lib/itcmdr-adminsvr) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_API_ADDR` | itcmdr-adminsvr | admin API listen address (default 0.0.0.0:8444) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_SITE_ADDR` | itcmdr-adminsvr | SiteLink listen address (default 0.0.0.0:8443) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_PUBLIC_API` | itcmdr-adminsvr | host:port clients use (put into enrollment codes) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_PUBLIC_SITE` | itcmdr-adminsvr | host:port sites use (put into site tokens) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_TLS_NAMES` | itcmdr-adminsvr | extra internal certificate names, comma separated |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_HEARTBEAT_SECS` | itcmdr-adminsvr | site status interval (default 60) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_SITE_MISSED` | itcmdr-adminsvr | missed heartbeats before a site is down (default 5) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_RP_ID` | itcmdr-adminsvr | WebAuthn RP id (default: the host of the public API) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_WEB_ORIGINS` | itcmdr-adminsvr | extra WebAuthn origins, comma separated |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT` | itcmdr-adminsvr | Firebase service account JSON (push to Android) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_FCM_URL` | itcmdr-adminsvr | FCM endpoint override |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_APNS_KEY` | itcmdr-adminsvr | APNs .p8 auth key file |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_APNS_KEY_ID` | itcmdr-adminsvr | APNs key id |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_APNS_TEAM_ID` | itcmdr-adminsvr | Apple team id |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_APNS_TOPIC` | itcmdr-adminsvr | the admin app's bundle id |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_APNS_URL` | itcmdr-adminsvr | APNs endpoint (sandbox for development builds) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_PUSH_MIN_SEVERITY` | itcmdr-adminsvr | lowest pushed severity (default cautious) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_DOMAINS` | itcmdr-adminsvr | Let's Encrypt names for the admin API, comma separated (empty = off) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_CONTACT` | itcmdr-adminsvr | ACME account contact e-mail(s) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_DIRECTORY` | itcmdr-adminsvr | production, staging or a directory URL (default production) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_DIRECTORY_ROOT` | itcmdr-adminsvr | PEM root for a test CA's directory |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_CHALLENGE` | itcmdr-adminsvr | tls-alpn-01 (default) or http-01 |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_HTTP_ADDR` | itcmdr-adminsvr | HTTP-01 listener (default 0.0.0.0:80) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_CACHE_DIR` | itcmdr-adminsvr | account key and certificate (default <data dir>/acme) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_RENEW_DAYS` | itcmdr-adminsvr | renew this many days before expiry (default 30) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_ACME_RETRY_SECS` | itcmdr-adminsvr | wait after a failed attempt (default 3600) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_BACKUP_DIR` | itcmdr-adminsvr | v1.2.0 folder for adminsvr's own backups (default <data dir>/backups; [adminsvr backup](BACKUP-ADMINSVR.md)) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_BACKUP_HOUR` | itcmdr-adminsvr | nightly backup hour, 0-23 local, or `off` (default 3) |\n| `ITCMDR_ADMINSVR_BACKUP_KEEP` | itcmdr-adminsvr | nightly backups kept (default 14; manual ones are never pruned) |\n\n## Set for plugins\n\n| Variable | Used by | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ITCMDR_PLUGIN_SECRET` | plugins | per-launch secret for the plugin protocol |\n| `ITCMDR_LOG_PATH` | plugins | the run's itcmdr.log/v1 file |\n| `ITCMDR_RUN_ID` | plugins | the run id |\n| `ITCMDR_MACHINE` | plugins | the machine name |\n| `ITCMDR_REPORT_ONLY` | plugins | 1 = do not repair |\n| `ITCMDR_ARTIFACT_DIR` | plugins | folder for files the plugin hands back |\n| `ITCMDR_SITE_PATH` | plugins | site.json for the PowerShell checks |\n| `ITCMDR_CHECKS_LIB` | plugins | path of Itcmdr.Checks.ps1 |\n| `ITCMDR_TCP_TIMEOUT_MS` | plugins | TCP check timeout override |\n| `ITCMDR_IMPORT_ONLY` | plugins | load the PowerShell agent's functions without running it |\n\n## Development and tests\n\n| Variable | Used by | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ITCMDR_TEST_SLOW` | tests | scale test deadlines (default 3) |\n| `ITCMDR_TEST_PG_URL` | tests | use this Postgres instead of starting one |\n| `ITCMDR_PG_BIN` | tests | Postgres binaries for tests (default ~/Projects/tools/pgsql/bin) |\n| `ITCMDR_REQUIRE_PG` | tests | fail instead of skipping without Postgres |\n| `ITCMDR_SKIP_PEBBLE` | tests | skip the Let's Encrypt (Pebble) test |\n| `ITCMDR_REGEN_VECTORS` | tests | regenerate signing vectors |\n| `ITCMDR_RESTORE_FORCE_HEALTH_FAIL` | tests | make the restore health check fail (rollback test) |\n| `ITCMDR_E2E_BIN_DIR` | tests | server binaries for the app's end-to-end test |\n| `ITCMDR_KEYS_BRIDGE_LIB` | tests | the app's Rust bridge library for Dart tests |\n| `ITCMDR_FAULT_ROOT` | tests | fault-injection suite root |\n| `ITCMDR_FAULT_SERVICE` | tests | service name for the fault suite |\n| `ITCMDR_FAULT_TESTBED` | tests | enables the fault suite (testbed machines only) |\n| `ITCMDR_PLUGINS_REPO` | scripts | plugins repository for fetch-plugins.sh |\n| `ITCMDR_LEAK_PATTERNS` | scripts | extra leak patterns for release-local.sh check |\n",
  "handover": "# itcmdr handover notes\n\nKeep this file current: update it at the start of each work item and in every commit. Phase statuses live in the root `PLAN.md` (update it in every commit too).\nPlaceholders only (SERVER01, WS-01, RCU-01, 198.51.100.x). Real site notes go in the\nuntracked mirror `HANDOVER.local.md` in the repo root (gitignored, never committed).\n\n## Current state (updated 2026-11-02)\n\n- **v1.1.0 released 2026-11-04 (tag v1.1.0):** Let's Encrypt for adminsvr (off by default, see docs/ADMINSVR.md \"HTTPS certificates and Let's Encrypt\"), HTTPS with certificate checks and per-server trust in both apps and the CLIs, site server API on HTTPS. See PLAN.md and docs/historical/1.0/SERVER-PLAN.md v1.1.0.\n- Branch: `main` only (no PRs, no feature branches). Latest tag: **v1.2.0** (adminsvr's own backup, real app ids). Previous: **v1.1.0** (HTTPS everywhere, Let's Encrypt for adminsvr, docs as in-app help). Previous: **v1.0.0** (1.0: fresh install with no share tested on the VM, README and INSTALL for 1.0, versions 1.0.0; deep-check reminder: the site keeps the date and result of the last `backup drill --deep`, sends only those to adminsvr, and the site app, `itcmdr-admin site list` and the admin app warn when never run, failed or older than the org limit (`org policy --deep-check-days`, default 90); v1.0.0 tests: Linux fmt/clippy clean, Rust 190 (SQLite+Postgres), Flutter 43 (+1 skipped)/26, Pester 105 (10 skipped); site branch updated with 1.0 links (not live); launch drafts in the site branch drafts/, never posted). Previous: v0.22.0 (share flows retired after the agent cutover: no schedule-state/agent-settings rewrites, on-demand runs as RunNow `*` for 0.22+ agents and per plugin for older ones, poller skips `requests\\`; on-site restore drill `backup drill [--deep]` + adminsvr `backup.drill` (ok/failed only) + monthly runbook in docs/BACKUP.md; fix for a server-upgrade rollback loop). v0.22.0 tests: Linux fmt/clippy clean, Rust 189+ (SQLite+Postgres), Flutter 41 (+1 skipped)/26, Pester 105 (10 skipped); Windows VM v22a/v22b: 0.21.0 install, agent pushed to 0.22.0-pre1 with the watchdog result reported 6 s after the new agent linked, rollout done, real server self-upgrade 0.21.0 -> 0.22.0-pre1, broken server release rolled back with the database restored, Critical alert and no re-staging (14/14 after the fix); VM restored from C:\\e2e\\v22-orig. Previous: v0.21.0 (agent and server self-upgrade with watchdogs and rollback; update window `updates.*` with push-now; approve-once releases from adminsvr (`release.rollout`, migration 000005); two-person approval as an org setting, off by default). v0.21.0 tests: Linux fmt/clippy clean, Rust 188 (SQLite+Postgres), Flutter 41 (+1 skipped)/26, Pester 104 (10 skipped); Windows VM v21a break tests on pre-releases: window holds, push installs at once, good upgrade, two broken releases rolled back, killed/stopped watchdog, stale agent, stopped server (25/26 checks; the 26th found the result-report race fixed in this release); VM restored from C:\\e2e\\v21-orig. Previous: v0.20.1 (agent ConfigUpdate over the link, gated `cutover switch --to agent` with rollback, nightly-task self-heal by the Rust agent and the PowerShell poller, idempotent switch). v0.20.1 tests: Linux fmt/clippy clean, Rust 166 (SQLite+Postgres), Flutter 38 (+1 skipped)/24, Pester 104 (10 skipped); Windows VM install test on a fresh test DB: config delivered, offline agent refused, real switch accepted (repeat is a no-op), real runs, re-enabled task disabled again with `nightly-task-reenabled`, tampered config.json rejected/alerted/resent without re-enabling the task, rollback re-enables the task and resolves the alert; the VM's original data restored afterwards. v0.21.0 details: docs/historical/1.0/SERVER-PLAN.md \"v0.21.0\". Next: v0.22.0, v1.0.0 (approved 2026-11-01). Previous: v0.20.0 (commands down with dry run, two-person approval, freeze, plugin catalog + FetchPackage, config/plugin documents, CLI + app UI; Flutter apps on FRB native assets + Swift Package Manager, no CocoaPods) (itcmdr Admin app in admin-app/ (Flutter + Rust bridge over admin-cli); FCM/APNs push with redacted payloads (stub-tested; accounts pending); metrics history; SysV/OpenRC/launchd/Windows service packaging; podman-tested container). Previous: v0.19.1 (adminsvr store on Diesel with SQLite (default) or Postgres; passkeys/security keys as an optional second factor with request-bound step-up, owner policy, reset, CLI via libfido2 tools, hmac-secret identity lock; real-key path untested). Previous: v0.19.0 (adminsvr core: `itcmdr-adminsvr` on Postgres with signed-request auth, owner/admin/sre RBAC, hash-chained audit, encrypted key backup on by default; `itcmdr-admin` CLI; site SiteLink `server/src/sitelink.rs` with deny-by-default redaction, dual-signed allowlisted commands `backup.start`/`alert.ack`/`alert.resolve`, no restore, kill switch `adminsvr.enabled`; see docs/ADMINSVR.md. adminsvr tests start a user-local Postgres from `ITCMDR_PG_BIN` (default ~/Projects/tools/pgsql/bin) or use `ITCMDR_TEST_PG_URL`. SRE app, push and a tested container image are v0.19.2). Previous: v0.18.0 (Rust agent runs plugins and schedules, report-only: `agent.plugins` selection pushed with a root-key endorsement and checked file checksums, PluginRemove, ScheduleSet fired by the agent also offline, RunNow `itcmdr-cli agent run/runs/plugins`, RunReports through the durable queue with artifacts, per-machine plugin logs and parity `agent:<run>` vs nightly checks, `agent-plugin:`/`agent-run:` alerts; `Prepare` RPC (refused root key leaves no enrollment), `Check` RPC (revoked agent backs off to 15 min); agent package ships itcmdr-plugin-ps.exe; schema 9; settings 49; versions 0.18.0, pubspec +30). v0.18.0 tests: Linux Rust 138, Flutter 38 (+1 skipped); Windows VM Rust 137, Pester 102, clippy clean; VM install test (v180s/v180f): plugin pushed and applied, RunNow report in 4 s with parity, schedule fired while the server was stopped and delivered after restart, a tampered server copy refused (checksum mismatch, alert raised then resolved), removal via `agent.plugins none`, tampered root leaves no PHCHECK row and the token still works, revoked agent logs refusal and backs off. v0.17.1: (agent certificates renewed over the link at 2/3 of `agent.certLifetimeMins` with a 24 h overlap and `agent-cert:<machine>` expiry alerts, `itcmdr-cli agent renew`; revoked/replaced client certificates refused in the TLS handshake by a serial allow-list in a custom acceptor; site CA signed by the root key, root public key in the token and verified by the agent; `agent.officeHours`; offline alert queue `itcmdr-agent alert` with durable seq, server dedupe and acks, `agent.queueMaxMib`; schema 8; versions 0.17.1, pubspec +29). v0.17.1 tests: Linux Rust 134, Flutter 38 (+1 skipped), Pester 102/112; Windows VM Rust 133 (restore_app rollback test hit a transient Access denied once, passed on rerun), Pester 102, clippy clean; VM install test (v171s): upgrade, CA signature file + root in token, tampered root refused, 60-min cert gives a Critical expiry alert, `agent renew` renews in ~5 s and resolves it, a revoked serial is refused in the TLS handshake (server log `handshake refused ... Revoked`), office hours keep a stopped agent at late with no alert outside the hours and alert inside, alerts queued while the server was stopped are delivered once after it starts and resolved via `--resolve`. v0.17.0: (Rust `itcmdr-agent` in the agent package: enrollment tokens, pinned TLS + mTLS client certs from a site CA, signed Hello, heartbeats + non-responsive alerts, maintenance, revoke, `itcmdr-cli agent`, app Agents page; schema 7; docs/AGENT.md; versions server/keys/app/installer/AgentVersion/agent 0.17.0). v0.17.0 tests: Linux Rust 129, Flutter 38 (+1 skipped), Pester 102/112 (fault suite skipped); Windows VM Rust 128, Pester 102, clippy clean; VM install test: server upgrade opens 50052 + firewall rule, agent package with /EnrollToken= enrolls the service in ~10 s and deletes the token, a wrong pin is refused, stopping the service gives late then non_responsive with a Critical alert, starting it resolves the alert, a CLI revoke ends the link at the next beat, re-enrollment works. v0.16.0: (site alerts: alerts tables, schema 6, ListAlerts/AckAlert/ResolveAlert/SilenceAlerts on the Itcmdr service, `itcmdr-cli alerts`, app Alerts page + badge; versions server/keys/app/installer/AgentVersion 0.16.0). v0.15.3: (generic names; history of itcmdr and itcmdr-plugins rewritten once more to scrub vendor/share names, tags re-pointed; re-clone old checkouts). v0.15.2: (site-specific values only in site.json, fault-injection suite, plugin errors for failing checks, app macos/). v0.15.1: Windows restore fix, real-server restore test, local release builds, ADMINSVR decisions 18 and 19. Previous: v0.15.0. Versions: server/keys/app/installer 0.15.1, itcmdr-common 0.15.0, itcmdr-plugin 0.11.0, AgentVersion 0.15.0, schema 5. Releases are built and published locally (`scripts/release-local.sh`, docs/RELEASE-LOCAL.md) while Actions is unavailable; Windows installers are unsigned (no code-signing certificate yet) and there is no macOS app archive until the app has a `macos/` project. v0.15.1: Linux Rust 120, Pester 96/96, Flutter 33 (1 skipped); Windows `restore_app` 10/10, clippy clean. Known: `cutover` test binary crashes at exit ~1% under load (pre-existing). Never run pre-v0.15.1 `restore_app` on a Windows box with itcmdr installed (it stops the real service).\n- Decisions (Michael, 2026-11-01): Windows installers and the macOS app stay unsigned until he gets signing; he installs podman on alien-blue himself (once `podman --version` works, test the adminsvr container image in v0.19.2); v0.19.2 scope approved (admin app, push notifications, tested container image). v0.19.1 = passkeys + Diesel (Postgres and SQLite) for adminsvr.\n- Before v0.15.0: Latest tag was **v0.14.3** (separate packages per component, the icon set in `branding/`). Previous: v0.14.2. Versions in the tree: server 0.14.3, itcmdr-keys 0.14.3, app 0.14.3, installer 0.14.3, agent `AgentVersion` 0.14.3. v0.14.3 Linux: fmt/clippy clean, Rust 104 passed, Pester 88/88, Flutter 31 passed (1 skipped). VM: all four packages build; upgrade from v0.14.2, fresh installs of each, server+app together, keep-data uninstalls and reinstall, full remove-data on a throwaway data dir all pass; the installed app shows the new icon. Windows app builds need a clean `build\\windows` after an icon change (the resource is cached). Host Flutter tests need `cd app/rust && cargo build` first. Run `scripts/fetch-plugins.sh` once after cloning (installer and parity tests need it).\n- Linux for v0.12.0: analyzer clean. Widget tests cover the Keys, Schedules and Backup dialogs and the settings search/switches. `app/test/e2e_test.dart` runs init, certifies an app key, then WhoAmI, every registry setting, a schedule, a backup and a restore dry run.\n- Windows VM passes: v0.10.1, v0.11.0, v0.12.0 and v0.13.0. App click-through on the VM: passed (Michael). The test VM runs signed-only. See Results.\n- **No install is switched.** Every install runs the SYSTEM task as before, plus the plugin versions report-only for parity. Switching is on site: collect a week of parity, `itcmdr-cli cutover status`, then `cutover vm-verified` and `cutover switch` (Michael).\n- GitHub release builds are blocked by org billing (see Risks), so there is no installer artifact yet.\n\n## Done (v0.10.0, plugin runtime + scheduler + restore server side)\n\n- `proto/plugin.proto` + `plugin/` crate `itcmdr-plugin` (SDK, host launch/handshake, `log` helpers + validator).\n- `server/src/runtime.rs` (run a plugin: Describe/Verify/Execute/Log, timeouts, plugin_runs, audit, share mirror),\n  `jobs.rs` (on-demand queue incl. plugins), `plugin_logs.rs` (ingest, rejects, metrics query), `schedule.rs`,\n  `restore.rs`; bins `itcmdr-plugin-ps` (PS host) and `itcmdr-restore` (helper). Schema 3.\n- `agent/Itcmdr.Plugin.psm1`; rotation zips `plugin-logs/` from finished weeks. Docs: `docs/LOG-STANDARD.md`,\n  `docs/BACKUP.md` (app restore), `docs/SETTINGS.md` (34 keys + server.json-only restore commands).\n- CLI: `schedule add|list|edit|rm|pause|resume|run-now|history`, `plugins run|runs`.\n- Tests: `server/tests/plugin_runtime.rs` (3), `schedule.rs` (4), `restore_app.rs` (1), schedule unit tests,\n  `agent/tests/Plugin.Tests.ps1` (6) + rotation plugin-logs test.\n\n## Done (v0.9.1, settings everywhere)\n\n- `server/src/settings.rs`: typed registry (29 keys), `resolve_all`, `set`/`unset` (global or per machine), `export_agent` \u2192 `<logs_dir>/agent-settings.json`. Reference: `docs/SETTINGS.md`.\n- RPCs `ListSettings` / `UpdateSetting` (admin/owner, audited); CLI `itcmdr-cli config list|get|set|unset [--machine]`.\n- Runtime reads settings per use (ingest interval, request timeout, retention, metrics, skew, search, package size).\n- Agent `Get-ItcmdrSetting` (site.json > central machine > central global > default), poller `Get-ItcmdrPollSetting`, installer reads `agent.nightlyTime`.\n- App: `lib/server_settings.dart` (basic screen in the Settings tab).\n- Plugins enabled on install by default (`--disabled`, setting `plugins.enable_on_install`).\n- Plan: decision 13 app-triggered restore (StageRestore + helper + rollback) in v0.10.0.\n- Tests: `server/tests/settings.rs` (2), CLI config test extended, packages test for enable-on-install, `agent/tests/Settings.Tests.ps1` (6), 2 Flutter tests.\n\n## Done (v0.9.0, packages)\n\n- `server/src/packages.rs`: `read_tar_gz`, `parse_manifest`, `validate`, `install` (quarantine on reject), `list`, `enable`, `disable`, `remove`, `pack`, `list_quarantine`.\n- `plugins` table (schema 2). CLI `plugins ...` / `service plugins ...`. Backups include `service_plugins/**` + `quarantine/index.jsonl`.\n- Tests: `server/tests/packages.rs` (5).\n\n## Done (v0.8.1, configurable paths)\n\n- Agent: `Get-ItcmdrPath` (site.json `paths.localLogs` / `agentData`), poller `Get-ItcmdrRequestDirectory`, rotation `Get-ItcmdrRotationRoot`; tests in `agent/tests/Paths.Tests.ps1`.\n- Server: `server/src/settings.rs` (DB overrides for `backup.*`), RPCs `GetSettings`/`UpdateSettings`, `itcmdr-cli config`. Retention: `backup::block_key`, `keep_blocks`.\n- Plans: decisions 14 and 15 in `docs/historical/1.0/SERVER-PLAN.md`.\n\n## Done (v0.8.0, itcmdr-cli + backup & restore)\n\n- `server/src/bin/itcmdr-cli.rs` (clap). `server/src/backup.rs`: `create`, `verify`, `open`, `list`, `prune_plan`/`prune`, `nightly`, `restore`.\n- `auth.rs`: `list_keys`, `certificate_of`, `rotate_root`. `db::SCHEMA_VERSION` (user_version). Config `backup` section.\n- RPCs `CreateBackup`/`ListBackups`/`VerifyBackup` in `api.rs`. Docs: `docs/BACKUP.md`, `docs/SIGNING.md`.\n- Tests: `server/tests/backup.rs` (6, incl. CLI end to end), `server/tests/signing.rs` (2).\n\n## Done (v0.7.0, keys & auth)\n\n- `server/src/keys.rs`: words, `Master::from_words`, `fingerprint`, `certify`/`verify_certificate`, `seal`/`unseal`, `request_message`.\n- `server/src/auth.rs`: `init`, `register`, `revoke`, `escrow_db_key`, `recover_db_key`, `load_root_key`, `sign_request`, `verify`, `require`, `NonceCache`, `Role`.\n- `server/src/api.rs`: `authz` (mutating) / `read_guard` (reads), `AuthMode`; config `auth_mode` (default `both`).\n- `server/src/main.rs`: `init`, `recover-db-key [--force]`. Tests: `server/tests/auth.rs` (4), unit tests in keys.rs/auth.rs.\n\n## Done (v0.6.2)\n\n- `db.rs::finish_migration` (read-back + count check, then `storage::shred_file`), `db::table_counts_of`. Tests: storage.rs (6), storage unit test for shred.\n\n## Done (v0.6.0, storage)\n\n- `server/src/storage.rs`: `resolve_data_dir` (flag > env > config > default), `Layout`, `write_secret`/`read_secret`\n  (DPAPI on Windows, 0600 hex elsewhere), `load_or_create_db_key`, `is_plaintext_sqlite`, `key_pragma`.\n- `server/src/db.rs`: `init_db` opens SQLCipher with `PRAGMA key` and errors clearly on a wrong key;\n  `migrate_plaintext` (sqlcipher_export + row-count check + `*.plaintext-backup`); roles/users/clients/server_meta; `role_id`.\n- `server/src/main.rs`: `--data-dir` in every mode. Tests: `server/tests/storage.rs` (5).\n- DPAPI code type-checked for Windows in a scratch crate (`cargo check --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnullvm`); not run on Windows.\n\n## Done (v0.5.0)\n\n- Agent: `Invoke-ItcmdrBackupCheck` + `Test-ItcmdrBackupTask` / `Test-ItcmdrBackupDestination`\n  (`site.backup.{expectedBackupSources -> BACKUP-SRC-A.., tasks, destinations}`; server role unless an entry lists `roles`).\n  `agent/Report-BackupCoverage.ps1` reuses them. Tests: `agent/tests/Backup.Tests.ps1`.\n- Poller: `Invoke-ItcmdrPoll -StaleLockMinutes` (default 150, above the task's 2h limit).\n- Server: `requests.rs::apply_timeout`, config `request_timeout_secs` (default 10800), status `TIMED_OUT`.\n- `server/tests/grpc.rs`: end-to-end over real gRPC (client codegen is now on in `server/build.rs`).\n\n## Done (v0.4.0)\n\n- `server/src/metrics.rs`: `count_flaps`, `flapping()` -> `flaps:<m>:<check>` (>= `FLAP_MIN`=3 changes);\n  `repeat_offender:<m>:<check>` (>= `REPEAT_MIN`=3 incidents). Test: `tests/ingest_metrics.rs::flapping_and_repeat_offenders`.\n- `server/src/search.rs::search`: machine/category/status exact (case-insensitive), text substring,\n  start/end on the record's ts, newest first, `limit` (default 100, max 1000) + `offset`; response `total`, `next_offset`.\n- App: `lib/charts.dart` (BarChart, `seriesByBucket`), `lib/logs_page.dart` (filters, date range, paging).\n\n## Done (v0.3.0, checks batch)\n\n- `agent/Invoke-ItcmdrAgent.ps1`: `Test-ItcmdrTcp` (TCP connect with timeout, replaces Test-NetConnection;\n  `ITCMDR_TCP_TIMEOUT_MS` overrides). Checks can return `ConvertTo-ItcmdrResult` (ok/detail/skipped);\n  `skipped` = no decision possible (e.g. nobody logged on), never repaired, no incident change.\n- `Invoke-ItcmdrScannerCheck` (category connectivity): `site.scanners[]` {name,address,ports,roles?} ->\n  `SCANNER-<name>-TCP-<port>`, report only. The 3D unit is allowed via an exception rule\n  (`checkPattern ^SCANNER-<name>-`, `downgrade:info`), see `config/site.example.json`.\n- `Invoke-ItcmdrConfigCheck` (category configuration):\n  - `dnsRecords[]` {name,expected[],forbidden[]} -> `DNS-<name>`; repair = local `ipconfig /flushdns` only.\n  - `driveMaps[]` {letter,path} -> `DRIVE-<L>`: reads each loaded user hive (HKU\\<sid>\\Network); report only.\n  - `printerQueues[]` {name,server,shared}: server role -> `PRNQ-<name>` (exists, shared, status, stuck jobs;\n    repair only removes jobs in error); workstations -> `PRNC-<name>` per-user connection; plus `SVC-Spooler`.\n  - ExampleVendor drift -> `EXAMPLEVENDOR-DRIFT-<file>`: files from `examplevendor.configFiles` (env vars + wildcards) searched for\n    `stalePatterns`, opened read-only with FileShare.ReadWrite. **No repair, ever.**\n- App: `configuration` added to on-demand categories. Tests: `agent/tests/Checks.Tests.ps1`.\n\n## Done (v0.2.0)\n\n- Server (`server/src`): idempotent timestamp-correct ingest incl. nested zips (`ingest.rs::ingest_all`),\n  incident dedup (`db.rs::update_incidents_for_check`), ISO-aligned metrics/rollups daily/weekly/\n  4-week block/ISO yearly (`metrics.rs`), Windows service + console mode (`main.rs`), on-demand\n  status from poller responses (`requests.rs`), exceptions format/validation/export\n  (`exceptions.rs`), token auth + audit log + ListAudit (`api.rs`), 400-day retention (`db.rs::apply_retention`).\n- Agent (`agent/`): PS 5.1-compatible; exceptions from site.json + server export; `-Categories`,\n  `-Repair`; poller with per-machine locks and responses; rotation weekly -> 4-week blocks -> ISO yearly\n  with verified manifests (`Rotate-ItcmdrLogs.ps1::Invoke-ItcmdrRotation`).\n- App (`app/lib`): Settings (host/port/TLS/token/actor), exceptions add/edit/expire, on-demand,\n  audit tab, ranked metrics tables, live refresh.\n- Installer (`installer/itcmdr.iss`): service with `--service`, config template, firewall, tasks, uninstall.\n- Sample data: `tools/sample-data-gen`.\n\n## Done (v0.11.0, built-in checks as plugins + default schedules)\n\n- `agent/Itcmdr.Checks.ps1` holds every check (moved, unchanged). `plugins/builtin/` had five plugins over it (moved to the itcmdr-plugins repo in v0.14.1) (network, scanners, config, backup-coverage, services); `Build-BuiltinPlugins.ps1` stages/signs them.\n- Agents run only the copies installed with the agent (`{app}\\agent\\plugins`); they never download plugins. Plugin packages are fetched (plain HTTPS) and run by the server only (decision 16).\n- `agent/Itcmdr.PluginRunner.ps1`: run plugins as child processes, `Compare-ItcmdrParity`, `schedule-state.json`, `cutover.json`, task enable/disable (never delete), local fallback. `Set-ItcmdrCutover.ps1 -To task|schedule|auto`.\n- Nightly: `agent.pluginMode` auto = plugins report-only first, then the built-in checks, then a `parity` line; after the switch the plugins are the run with per-plugin fallback to the built-in function. run-start carries `trigger`, `cutover`, `scheduleStateTs`, `pluginMode`.\n- Server: `cutover.rs` (seed `Nightly agent run` paused + `Nightly backup`, reset-to-default, remembered deletes, gate, switch, rollback, `schedule-state.json` each pass); backup loop replaced by the backup schedule; schema 4 (`parity_runs`, `agent_state`); gRPC `ResetScheduleToDefault`, `ScheduleRef.confirm`, `Schedule.builtin_key/action` (Dart protos regenerated).\n- CLI: `schedule reset-to-default`, `schedule rm --force --confirm \"<name>\"`, `cutover status|vm-verified|switch|rollback`.\n\n## Done (v0.12.0, the app signs requests; released as eea653d)\n\nBase v0.11.0 = `c0748ea`. WIP `aa5b408`, released `eea653d` (tag v0.12.0). Linux at the WIP commit: cargo fmt/clippy -D warnings clean, Rust 93 passed,\nPester 82/82, flutter test 15 passed, flutter analyze 4 infos (curly braces in backup_page.dart, schedules_page.dart).\nLater the same day: those 4 infos fixed, widget tests added, Dart e2e green, versions set to 0.12.0. Analyzer clean.\n\n**Done (server, tested on Linux)**\n- Sealed restore words: `server/src/restore_seal.rs` (X25519 + HKDF (SHA-256) + XChaCha20-Poly1305, one-time 10-min keys bound to the signing key, aad binds key id + backup). `GetRestoreKey` RPC; `StageRestore` refuses plaintext `words`, needs `sealed_words`, `dry_run` first, then `dry_run_id` (30 min, same key + backup). Restore/keys RPCs are signed-only (`signed_only` in `api.rs`); the shared token cannot restore. Audited: restore-dry-run, restore-refused, restore-stage. `itcmdr-cli restore-stage` uses the same path. Tests: `server/tests/restore_app.rs`.\n- New RPCs: WhoAmI, ListKeys (admin), RegisterKey/RevokeKey (owner; bundle certified offline), ListPlugins, PreviewSchedule. Tests: `server/tests/app_api.rs`.\n- auth_mode: default `signed`. Without a value in server.json, `db::init_db` writes `server_meta.auth_mode_default` once (\"both\" if the DB already had tables = pre-0.12 install, else \"signed\"); `auth::settle_auth_mode` applies it in `lib.rs run`. Unknown/empty mode fails secure (Signed). Installer template no longer sets auth_mode (existing installs keep their explicit \"both\"). `itcmdr-cli auth status|set-mode MODE [--force]` (refuses `signed` without a non-root admin/owner key).\n- Vectors: `proto/signing_vectors.json` (+3 new, old ones unchanged), new `proto/app_vectors.json` (Ed25519 sig + sealed words).\n\n**Done (app, Linux)**\n- `app/lib/signing.dart`: AppKey, SigningInterceptor, `canonicalBytes` (Dart writes explicit defaults; the vector test caught it), `sealWords`. `app/test/signing_test.dart` matches the Rust vectors byte for byte.\n- Pages: `keys_page.dart`, `schedules_page.dart`, `backup_page.dart`, `plugins_page.dart`; `main.dart` now a NavigationRail with 11 entries; `settings.dart` stores the key seed (`auth.key_seed`). The Keys page shows the local fingerprint before the server answers.\n- Settings: search box and a switch for each bool (`server_settings.dart`). `app/test/pages_test.dart` checks every key in `docs/SETTINGS.md` plus the dialogs. `app/test/e2e_test.dart` checks the live registry against that doc.\n\n**Half-done / still open**\n- Plugin install and enable/disable are CLI-only. The app lists, runs and charts.\n- Key seed is stored in shared_preferences (user profile, plaintext); DPAPI later.\n- VM: v0.12.0 upgrade is installed (Pester 82/82, service Running, tasks Ready, app binary matches the build). `auth status` is `both` from server.json. The screens were not clicked through.\n\n**Next steps in order**\n1. Click through the installed app on the VM (connect, Keys, Schedules, Backup dry run, Settings) with a person at the screen.\n2. A real second PC as an agent.\n3. On site (Michael): the signed-only checklist in docs/INSTALL.md; a week of parity, then the gated cutover.\n4. Done in v0.14.1: plugins in itcmdr-plugins. The itcmdr history was rewritten once to drop `plugins/` (approved by Michael). Re-clone, or `git fetch --force --tags origin && git reset --hard origin/main`.\n\n**Commands**\n- Linux: `export PATH=\$HOME/Projects/tools/flutter/bin:\$HOME/Projects/tools/pwsh:\$HOME/Projects/tools/protoc/bin:\$HOME/.pub-cache/bin:\$PATH`; `cargo fmt --all --check && cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings && cargo test --workspace`; `pwsh -NoProfile -c 'Invoke-Pester -Path agent/tests -CI'`; `cd app && flutter analyze && flutter test`.\n- Protos: `protoc --dart_out=grpc:app/lib/proto -Iproto proto/itcmdr.proto` (Rust regenerates in build.rs). Vectors: `ITCMDR_REGEN_VECTORS=1 cargo test --test signing` (add only, never change existing).\n- VM: see HANDOVER.local.md.\n\n**Gotchas**\n- pwsh on Linux crashed after the reboot (\"The given assembly name was invalid\"): a corrupt `~/.cache/powershell/StartupProfileData-NonInteractive`. Moving the cache aside fixed it.\n- One `corrupted size vs. prev_size` abort at exit of the backup test binary, not reproduced in 3 reruns.\n- Tests that use the shared token must set `auth_mode: \"both\"` (default is now signed).\n\n## In progress\n\nv1.1.0 (not tagged): HTTPS default / ACME / trust work is on main. Test flakes under load were fixed in 2026-11-04: wall-clock deadlines (`ITCMDR_TEST_SLOW`, default 3), retries while the API or Pebble starts, and `srv::run` closing its pool on stop (the calllog test binary used to segfault at exit when SQLite workers were still closing). Tests that start a server should stop it and await it before returning. The app e2e test finds the server binaries under `\$CARGO_TARGET_DIR/debug` when that is set (or `ITCMDR_E2E_BIN_DIR`). After moving the checkout, rerun `flutter pub get` in both apps (`.dart_tool` keeps absolute paths) and `cargo clean -p itcmdr-keys` if a shared target dir holds test binaries built at the old path (`env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\")` is baked in). 2026-11-04: full suite green, main pushed (no tag). Docs rule from now on: update docs/ in the same commit as the code; after changing a CLI flag rebuild and run `scripts/gen-man.py`, after changing docs run `scripts/gen-help.py` (both have `--check`). Next: Windows VM check, then release.\n\nNothing in progress. Proposed next: server/agent + adminsvr architecture (docs/historical/1.0/design/SERVER-AGENT.md, docs/historical/1.0/design/ADMINSVR.md incl. the SRE Flutter app; roadmap v0.15.0 to v0.22.0 in PLAN.md), Michael's decisions recorded 2026-10-31 (incl. key-based adminsvr auth and the signed, hash-chained audit of every change) (ADMINSVR.md \"Decisions\"); next is v0.14.2 (extract `itcmdr-keys` from server/src/keys.rs, auth.rs and restore_seal.rs; app/lib/signing.dart moves to flutter_rust_bridge), then v0.15.0. Passkeys/YubiKeys (second factor) planned as v0.19.1. Key backup defaults decided (on, 24-word lock, COOP/COEP fine); v0.14.2 released (keys crate + bridge; VM pass below). v0.15.0 released (errors + calls). Next: v0.16.0 (alerts). Pending: Mac signing step for iOS/macOS, the adminsvr agent design, rootless Podman install on alien-blue (sudo needs a password: `sudo pacman -S --needed podman passt slirp4netns fuse-overlayfs podman-compose`; subuid/subgid for m3talsmith already set, 100000:65536). v0.14.1 released; itcmdr history rewritten once (plugins/ removed, tags recreated); VM tree re-synced from the new history.\n\n## Backlog (in order)\n\n1. Windows VM test pass: v0.10.1 to v0.14.0 done (results below). App click-through passed (Michael). Still open: a real second PC as agent.\n2. ~~Missing agent checks~~ done in v0.3.0. Real values must be added to the site.json on Windows hosts.\n3. ~~Flapping/repeat-offender metrics, charts~~ done in v0.4.0.\n4. ~~Log search filters and paging~~ done in v0.4.0.\n5. ~~Backup coverage~~ done in v0.5.0. Real task names/destinations: see HANDOVER.local.md \u2192 Real-site notes.\n6. ~~Stale locks, request timeouts~~ done in v0.5.0.\n7. ~~gRPC integration test~~ done in v0.5.0.\n8. Unblock GitHub Actions (billing) or build the installer on a Windows machine.\n\n## Build and test (Linux dev box)\n\nTools are under `~/Projects/tools` (flutter, pwsh, protoc); add them to PATH:\n`export PATH=\$HOME/Projects/tools/flutter/bin:\$HOME/Projects/tools/pwsh:\$HOME/Projects/tools/protoc/bin:\$HOME/.pub-cache/bin:\$PATH`\n\n```\nscripts/fetch-plugins.sh   # pinned itcmdr-plugins -> third_party (installer + parity tests)\ncargo build --workspace --all-targets && cargo test --workspace\ncargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\n(cd app && flutter pub get && flutter analyze && flutter test)\npwsh -NoProfile -c 'Invoke-ScriptAnalyzer -Path agent -Recurse -Severity Warning,Error'\npwsh -NoProfile -c '\$c=New-PesterConfiguration;\$c.Run.Path=\"agent/tests\";Invoke-Pester -Configuration \$c'\npython3 scripts/scan_repo.py        # leak scanner (also the pre-commit hook)\n```\n\n- Dart protos: `protoc -I proto --dart_out=grpc:app/lib/proto proto/itcmdr.proto` (protoc_plugin 25.1.0).\n- Rust protos regenerate automatically via `server/build.rs`.\n- The Windows service code can't be fully cross-compiled here (SQLite needs Windows headers);\n  type-check it by copying `main.rs` into a scratch crate and running `cargo check --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnullvm`.\n\n## Release procedure\n\n1. All the checks above are green, and `scan_repo.py` is clean.\n2. Grep the staged diff against `config/banned.local.txt`; it must have 0 hits.\n3. Commit on `main`. Bump `MyAppVersion` (installer), `version` in `server/Cargo.toml` and `app/pubspec.yaml`, `AgentVersion` when the agent changed; add a `CHANGELOG.md` entry.\n4. `git tag -a vX.Y.Z -m \"itcmdr vX.Y.Z\"`, then `git push origin main`, then `git push origin vX.Y.Z`.\n5. Never force-push, and never move or delete tags. Push only tested, tagged commits.\n6. The tag triggers `.github/workflows/release.yml`, which builds the Windows installer once Actions works.\n7. Until then (v0.15.0 and later): build and publish locally from the tag with `scripts/release-local.sh` (Linux and macOS: `unix`; Windows: the VM step; then `check` and `publish`). See `docs/RELEASE-LOCAL.md`.\n\n## Windows VM test plan\n\n1. Install with `/DAllowSubnet=<lab subnet>`; `sc qc itcmdr-server` shows `\"\u2026\\itcmdr-server.exe\" --service`;\n   the service is Running; `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr\\server-logs\\itcmdr-server.txt` exists; it survives a stop/start and a reboot.\n2. Set `logs_dir` and `admin_token_hash` (`itcmdr-server.exe hash-token <t>`) in `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr\\server.json`,\n   then restart; the app connects; with no token in the app, a trigger gives the clear error.\n3. `powershell.exe -File Invoke-ItcmdrAgent.ps1 -ReportOnly` under **Windows PowerShell 5.1**: the JSONL log\n   appears, is copied to the share, and is queued when the share is offline.\n4. Start the nightly and poller tasks manually as SYSTEM; the poller repeats every minute and skips overlaps.\n5. On-demand: one machine, then all machines; status moves queued -> running -> done.\n6. An exception added in the app appears in `<share>\\exceptions.json` and is honoured on the next run.\n6a. v0.9.1: change `agent.quietMinutes` for one PC in the app; `<share>\\agent-settings.json` updates, the PC uses it on the\n   next run (and from the agentData cache with the share offline); `itcmdr-cli config list --machine <PC>` agrees.\n6b. v0.10.0: `itcmdr-cli plugins run <name>` with a PowerShell plugin under Windows PowerShell 5.1; a schedule\n   (`itcmdr-cli schedule add ... --when \"every 1h\"`) queues runs; app-triggered restore: StageRestore stops and\n   restarts the service via sc.exe and rolls back on a failed health check.\n7. Rotation with `-Root <copy> -Today 2027-01-04`: the zips open in Explorer, and the server ingests the archive.\n8. Uninstall removes the service, the tasks and the firewall rule.\n9. v0.11.0: after an upgrade, `itcmdr-cli schedule list` shows the two built-ins; the SYSTEM nightly writes a `parity`\n   line with 0 mismatches/errors; `schedule run-now \"Nightly agent run\"` reaches the agent with `trigger=schedule`;\n   `Set-ItcmdrCutover.ps1 -To schedule|task|auto` disables/re-enables the task; an agent reinstall keeps it disabled;\n   with the server stopped the poller runs the nightly locally (`trigger=fallback`); `cutover switch` is refused\n   without a clean week. Never run `cutover switch` on a real install without the gate.\n\n### Results: first VM pass (v0.10.1, 2026-10-30, Windows 11 Pro, Windows PowerShell 5.1)\n\nBuilt on the VM (Rust MSVC, Flutter, Inno Setup), installed silently with the full set of components, and used a local folder shared as `DATA` to stand in for the share. Placeholder office values only.\n\n| Item | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Pester 65/65 and PSScriptAnalyzer 0 findings under PS 5.1 (Pester 6.2) | \u2705 after fixing the tests' 3-argument `Join-Path` |\n| Installer compiles | \u2705 after fixes (brace escapes, quoting, `[Code]` comments, `target\\release` path) |\n| Silent install: service Running (delayed-auto, under `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr`), 3 tasks, firewall rule, files | \u2705 |\n| Service starts again after a reboot | \u2705 |\n| `hash-token` + restart; `itcmdr-cli init` (words shown once), `user add`, `keys list` | \u2705 |\n| Nightly task (SYSTEM): log written, copied to the share | \u2705 |\n| Ingest of agent logs | \u274c \u2192 fixed: PS 5.1 wrote a UTF8 byte-order mark, so the run-start line was lost (fixed in both the agent and the server) |\n| On-demand `TriggerRuns` \u2192 request file \u2192 poller \u2192 DONE | \u2705 (~60 s) |\n| Plugin `pack` / `install` (enabled) / `run` through `itcmdr-plugin-ps` on PS 5.1 (CLI and the SYSTEM service via `RunPlugin`) | \u2705 |\n| A failed Verify skips Execute; `plugins runs`; v1 log + share mirror; `GetPluginMetrics` | \u2705 |\n| Schedules: minute cron fired, the concurrency skip worked, pause/rm, next runs with timezones | \u2705 |\n| Central agent settings per machine \u2192 `agent-settings.json` on the share \u2192 the agent sees the value | \u2705 |\n| Backup create, list, verify, verify --deep | \u2705 |\n| Rotation task: weekly zip including `plugin-logs/` | \u2705. The server read those zipped plugin logs as agent logs \u274c \u2192 fixed |\n| In-place upgrade over a running install | \u2705 |\n| `restore-stage` (new CLI on the StageRestore path) \u2192 sc.exe stop/start \u2192 done; wrong words refused | \u2705 |\n| Forced health failure \u2192 rolled back, service running, old value back | \u2705 |\n| Uninstall removes the service, tasks, firewall rule and program dir, and keeps the data; reinstall picks it up | \u2705 |\n| Agent repairs on a VM with the example site.json | \u26a0\ufe0f NET-IP-CORRECT set the example DNS server and cut the VM off. Expected with wrong site values: site.json must match the real network before repairs run |\n\n### Results: second VM pass (v0.11.0, 2026-10-30, Windows 11 Pro, Windows PowerShell 5.1)\n\nInstaller built on the VM and installed over v0.10.1 (in-place upgrade). Placeholder office values only; no switch.\n\n| Item | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Pester 81/81 and PSScriptAnalyzer 0 findings under PS 5.1 (incl. plugin parity and end-to-end runs) | \u2705 |\n| Upgrade: service Running, new agent files and `agent\\plugins\\<5>\\main.ps1` installed | \u2705 |\n| Default schedules seeded: `Nightly agent run` (paused, 01:30, repair) and `Nightly backup` (on, 02:00, catch-up); `schedule-state.json` on the share | \u2705 |\n| SYSTEM nightly: built-in run + plugins report-only first; parity 17 matched, 0 mismatched, 0 errors; itcmdr.log/v1 plugin logs | \u2705 |\n| `cutover status` shows 7 empty days and the blocking reasons; `cutover switch` refused | \u2705 |\n| `schedule run-now \"Nightly agent run\"` \u2192 poller \u2192 agent with `trigger=schedule`, parity 17/0 | \u2705 |\n| `Set-ItcmdrCutover.ps1 -To schedule` disables the task; `-To task` re-enables it; `-To auto` follows the server | \u2705 |\n| Agent reinstall keeps the disabled task disabled | \u274c \u2192 fixed: the agent's `\$Trigger` parameter collided with the installer script's `\$trigger` (re-registration failed); now \u2705 |\n| Server stopped \u2192 schedule state stale \u2192 poller ran the nightly locally (`trigger=fallback`, plugins mode, 17 checks) | \u2705 (~150 s with serverStaleMinutes=2 for the test) |\n| Share offline (SMB share removed): log queued in `spool\\outbox`; share back \u2192 flushed to the share | \u2705 |\n| \"local\" schedule times on Windows across the fall-back day | \u274c \u2192 fixed: 02:00 showed 01:00 -08:00 on Nov 1; now uses the IANA zone |\n| App UI on Windows; a real second PC | \u23f3 app binary installed 2026-10-31; the screens were not clicked through. A second PC is still not done. |\n\n### Results: v0.12.0 VM pass (2026-10-31, Windows 11 Pro, Windows PowerShell 5.1)\n\nIn-place upgrade over the v0.11.0 install. The installer is built after `flutter build windows` (the first package that morning was built before the app binary existed, so it still contained the previous app; it was rebuilt and installed again).\n\n| Item | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Release build of the server | \u2705 1m 55s, exit 0 (one duplicated warning) |\n| Installer compiles | \u2705 |\n| Pester 82/82 and PSScriptAnalyzer 0 findings under PS 5.1 | \u2705 |\n| `flutter build windows` | \u2705 79s |\n| Silent upgrade: exit 0, service Running, nightly / poller / rotation tasks still Ready | \u2705 |\n| Installed agent `AgentVersion` 0.12.0; installed `itcmdr.exe` timestamp matches the build | \u2705 |\n| `itcmdr-cli auth status` | both, because server.json still has `\"auth_mode\": \"both\"` from the v0.11.0 installer. Upgrades do not replace that file, so this install does not take the kept-both path. The shared token is still configured. One admin key (`Dr Example`) can sign. |\n| Kept-both path: `auth_mode` removed from server.json, service restarted | \u2705 `auth status` = both (\"not set; this install predates v0.12.0\"), unsigned token call still works; server.json restored after |\n| Installed `itcmdr.exe` starts and stays up (15 s, headless) | \u2705 |\n| Dart e2e against a real server on Windows (`flutter test test\\e2e_test.dart`, release bins via `ITCMDR_E2E_BIN_DIR`) + signing and page tests | \u2705 14 passed |\n| Dart e2e on Windows | \u274c \u2192 fixed: the test hard-coded `../target/debug/itcmdr-server` (no `.exe`, no bin dir override) |\n| App screens clicked through on the VM | \u2705 passed (Michael, 2026-10-31) |\n\n### Results: v0.15.0 VM pass (2026-10-31, Windows 11 Pro, Windows PowerShell 5.1)\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Release build, Pester, Flutter tests, Dart e2e (release bins) | \u2705 96/96, 33 passed (1 skipped), e2e passed |\n| `cargo test --workspace` on Windows (first time run there) | \u26a0\ufe0f 118 passed, 1 failed: `restore_app` (Access is denied on the open DB; fails identically on v0.14.3) |\n| Four 0.15.0 packages build (ISCC) | \u2705 |\n| In-place upgrade 0.14.3 \u2192 0.15.0 (server, agent, app, cli) | \u2705 service Running, keys/config unchanged, signed-only kept, AgentVersion 0.15.0 |\n| Signed app calls logged; `itcmdr-cli calls list/show`, `--json` | \u2705 principal = key name + fingerprint |\n| Plugin run after upgrade | \u2705 itcmdr-network ok |\n\n### Results: v0.14.2 VM pass (2026-10-31, Windows 11 Pro, Windows PowerShell 5.1)\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Release build of the server bins with `itcmdr-keys` | \u2705 |\n| `cargo test -p itcmdr-keys` on Windows (vectors) | \u2705 8 passed |\n| `flutter build windows` (native assets build the bridge (v0.20.0; cargokit before); `itcmdr_keys_bridge.dll` next to `itcmdr.exe`) | \u2705 |\n| Installer (ISCC) and upgrade over v0.14.1 | \u2705 exit 0, service Running, `AgentVersion` 0.14.2, bridge DLL installed, signed mode kept |\n| Pester (Windows PowerShell 5.1) / PSSA | \u2705 88/88, 0 findings |\n| flutter test (incl. DPAPI and e2e against the release bins) | \u2705 31 passed, 1 skipped |\n| Installed app starts and stays up 15 s | \u2705 |\n| Signed calls with the DPAPI-stored admin key through the bridge (WhoAmI, ListSchedules, ListPlugins) | \u2705 |\n\nWeb: the `itcmdr-keys` crate and the bridge build for wasm32 (`flutter_rust_bridge_codegen build-web`), and the crate passes the pinned vectors under WASM (`wasm-pack test --node keys`). The on-site app itself is desktop-only and does not compile for web (its `key_store.dart` imports `dart:ffi` for Windows DPAPI, as before), so no web build of the on-site app ships. A Flutter in-browser test (`flutter test --platform chrome`) hung loading `pkg/` and is a follow-up for the SRE app, which targets web.\n\n### Results: v0.14.1 VM pass (2026-10-31, plugins from itcmdr-plugins)\n\n| Item | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Build, ISCC with the pinned copy, Pester 88/88 (parity against the pinned plugins), PSScriptAnalyzer 0, `flutter test` 31 + 1 skipped | \u2705 |\n| Upgrade to v0.14.1: installed `agent\\plugins` = the five pinned copies (hash equal), `AgentVersion` 0.14.1, signed mode kept | \u2705 |\n| itcmdr-plugins Pester on PS 5.1 | \u2705 10/10 after a test fix (v1.0.1) |\n| `Install-Plugins.ps1 -Disabled` against the signed-mode server (registered admin key) | \u2705 five installed disabled |\n| Remove, then `Install-Plugins.ps1` (enable on install) | \u2705 five enabled |\n| Tampered package via `-Packages` | \u2705 refused (\"sha256 does not match\"), quarantined, exit 1 |\n| `-Cli` pointing nowhere | \u2705 \"itcmdr-cli not found\", exit 1 |\n| `itcmdr-cli plugins run <check plugin>` | \u274c \u2192 fixed: \"plugin exited before the handshake\" (verbatim `\\\\?\\` script path, so `\$PSScriptRoot` was empty under PS 5.1). After the fix all five run (verify ok, execute ok). They ran in repair mode on the test VM. |\n\n### Results: v0.14.0 VM pass (2026-10-31, Windows 11 Pro, Windows PowerShell 5.1)\n\n| Item | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Build, installer compile, Pester 88/88, PSScriptAnalyzer 0, `flutter test` 31 + 1 skipped | \u2705 (3 PSSA findings in the new script, fixed) |\n| Back up `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr` and `%APPDATA%\\itcmdr` (robocopy, service stopped), silent uninstall, data removed | \u2705 |\n| Clean silent install with `/LogsDir /BackupDir /SiteName /ServerHostname /Share` | \u2705 exit 0, service Running, values in server.json and site.json, `setup-check.txt` all OK except \"not initialized\" (warn), agent check all OK |\n| No words in the installer log | \u2705 |\n| `setup first-run` with redirected output | \u2705 refused |\n| `setup first-run --app-key-store --admin-name` (headless test flag) | \u2705 24 words, first admin (owner) registered, key created in the DPAPI store |\n| The Dart app store reads the key the CLI wrote; signed WhoAmI (owner), ListSchedules, ListPlugins; token refused | \u2705 signed from day one |\n| Installed app starts; agent poller result 0 | \u2705 |\n| Uninstall, restore the backup, reinstall v0.13.0, upgrade to v0.14.0 with different site values | \u2705 server.json byte-identical, site.json unchanged, `setup apply` not run, tasks Ready, signed calls work, token refused, cutover state unchanged |\n| Interactive console (pause, ask 3 words, re-show on mismatch, clear screen) | \u2705 on Linux through a pseudo-terminal. On Windows it uses `cls`; the console window itself still needs a person at the screen |\n\n### Results: v0.13.0 VM pass (2026-10-31, Windows 11 Pro, Windows PowerShell 5.1)\n\nIn-place upgrade over v0.12.0.\n\n| Item | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Release build, `flutter build windows`, installer compile | \u2705 |\n| Pester 82/82, PSScriptAnalyzer 0 findings (PS 5.1) | \u2705 |\n| `flutter test` on Windows (all files, incl. the DPAPI round trip and tamper check; Dart e2e incl. plugin upload/quarantine against release bins) | \u2705 31 passed, 1 skipped (secret-tool, Linux only) |\n| Silent upgrade: exit 0, service Running, tasks Ready, `AgentVersion` 0.13.0 | \u2705 |\n| App key in the DPAPI store (`%APPDATA%\\itcmdr\\app-key.dpapi`), no plaintext copy | \u2705 (the VM has no words any more, so the registered `Dr Example` admin key was imported into the store) |\n| Signed WhoAmI / ListSchedules / ListPlugins in `both` | \u2705 |\n| `auth set-mode signed` + restart: signed calls work; token and unauthenticated calls refused (`Unauthenticated`, \"missing x-itcmdr-key\") | \u2705 |\n| Rollback `auth set-mode both` + restart: token calls work again, signed still work | \u2705 |\n| Back to `signed`; agent poller run result 0; installed app starts and stays up | \u2705 |\n| Final state | VM left in `auth_mode = signed` |\n\n## Risks and gotchas\n\n- **GitHub Actions is blocked by billing** on the WeirdBrains org (\"payments failed / spending limit\").\n  The repo is private, so Windows minutes are billed.\n- **The Actions allowlist is set at the org level.** Only GitHub-owned actions are allowed, so the\n  workflows use no third-party actions (keep it that way, or have an org admin allow them).\n- **PowerShell 5.1:** the scheduled tasks run `powershell.exe` (5.1). Don't use `ConvertFrom-Json -AsHashtable`,\n  `[System.Globalization.ISOWeek]`, three-argument `Join-Path` (fine in tests only), or other PS7-only features.\n  Use `Get-ItcmdrValue` for config lookups; StrictMode throws on missing keys.\n- Real site values and instructions: see HANDOVER.local.md \u2192 Real-site notes.\n- **Never commit real site values** (IPs, hostnames, domains, usernames, MACs).\n  `config/site.local.json`, `config/site.json` and `config/banned.local.txt` are gitignored.\n- The scanner allows only `0.0.0.0`, `TCP-<port>` and `SCAN-NN` tokens beyond the placeholders; don't widen it further.\n- Mutating RPCs need a signed request (v0.12.0 default) or, on an install that still has `auth_mode = both`, the legacy token. Run `itcmdr-server init` once per server; the 24 words are\n  printed ONCE and are the only way to certify new keys and recover `db.key`. New installs are `signed`. An upgrade whose server.json does not set `auth_mode` keeps `both` until `itcmdr-cli auth set-mode signed` (needs a non-root admin or owner key first). A value already in server.json wins: the v0.11.0 installer wrote `both`, and upgrades do not replace that file.\n- Never change the master derivation (`keys.rs`); the pinned test vector guards it.\n- Never edit `proto/signing_vectors.json` to make a test pass; regenerate (ITCMDR_REGEN_VECTORS=1) only for a deliberate protocol change.\n- Agent paths come from `Get-ItcmdrPath`; never hard-code `C:\\maintenanceRuns` again. In tests use `[IO.Path]::Combine` (Join-Path fails on drive letters / UNC on Linux).\n- Use `settings::backup_dir` / `effective_backup` (not `cfg.backup` directly): DB overrides win.\n- Package installs do file IO and downloads in `spawn_blocking` (ureq is blocking); keep it that way.\n- Never loosen `packages::safe_rel_path` / the regular-file-only rule; restore reuses it for archived plugin paths.\n- Bump `db::SCHEMA_VERSION` with every schema change: restore uses it to refuse archives from newer versions.\n- Tests that start the server should set `backup.nightly = false` (the loop would otherwise write a backup into the temp dir).\n- **SQLCipher build** needs perl + a C compiler (vendored OpenSSL); GitHub Windows runners have Strawberry Perl.\n  The first build takes ~40 s on alien-blue.\n- **Losing `keys/db.key` means losing the database** (it is random, not derived from the words). Back up the\n  data dir's `keys/` with the server backup; on Windows the DPAPI blob only decrypts on the same machine.\n- Since v0.6.2 the `*.plaintext-backup` is overwritten and deleted automatically after a verified read-back\n  (`db.rs::finish_migration`). If verification fails it is KEPT (unencrypted) and an error is logged: check the\n  server log and delete it by hand once resolved.\n- Never put `;` inside the SQL comments in `db.rs::create_schema` (the schema is split on `;`).\n\n## Where real config lives (not in git)\n\n- The dev box: `config/site.local.json` and `config/banned.local.txt` in the repo (gitignored), plus `HANDOVER.local.md`.\n- Windows hosts: `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\site.json` and `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\server.json`.\n\n\n## Fault-injection suite (v0.15.2)\n\nOn a disposable Windows test bed only: `powershell -File scripts\\Invoke-ItcmdrFaultSuite.ps1 -IAmATestBed [-Service <harmless service>] [-Root <scratch>]`. It refuses without `-IAmATestBed`, restores the service and removes the scratch folder. Never run it on a production server.\n",
  "help": "# In-app help\n\nThese guides are the help: the same files are built into the programs, so the docs and the\nhelp never differ.\n\n- **Site app and admin app:** the Help page (site app: the Help entry in the menu; admin app:\n  Settings > Help). Pick a topic or search titles and text.\n- **itcmdr-cli and itcmdr-admin:** `help` lists topics; `help <topic>` prints one, for example\n  `itcmdr-cli help backup` or `itcmdr-admin help certificates`. Topic names are the file names in\n  lower case without `.md`. Command help is still `--help`.\n\nThe apps embed a generated copy (`lib/help_topics.g.dart`, from `scripts/gen-help.py`); a test fails\nwhen it differs from `docs/`. Archived pages in `docs/historical/` are not help topics.\n",
  "install": "# Install guide (admin PowerShell)\n\nThis repository contains placeholders only. Real values go in `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\site.json` (start from `config/site.example.json`) and `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\server.json`. Never commit real values.\n\n## Packages (v0.14.3)\n\nEach part has its own installer, with its own entry in Apps & features and its own uninstaller.\nThey all install under `C:\\Program Files\\itcmdr\\<part>` and share the data folder `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr`.\n\n| Package | File | What it installs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Server | `itcmdr-server-Setup-<ver>.exe` | the `itcmdr-server` service, its own copy of `itcmdr-cli`, `itcmdr-plugin-ps`, `itcmdr-restore`, log rotation, firewall rule |\n| Agent | `itcmdr-agent-Setup-<ver>.exe` | the PowerShell agent, the pinned check plugins, the nightly and poller tasks, `Push-Install-ItcmdrAgent.ps1` |\n| CLI | `itcmdr-cli-Setup-<ver>.exe`, `itcmdr-cli-<ver>-linux-x86_64.tar.gz`, `itcmdr-cli-<ver>-macos-<arch>.tar.gz` | `itcmdr-cli` on PATH (Windows: `C:\\Program Files\\itcmdr\\cli`) |\n| App | `itcmdr-app-Setup-<ver>.exe` (plus Linux and macOS builds from CI) | the desktop app with the `itcmdr_keys_bridge.dll` crypto bridge |\n\n`SHA256SUMS` on the release page lists every file.\n\n- **The server and the CLI:** the server package always carries its own `itcmdr-cli` in `server\\`. The installer\n  needs it for setup, so the server works without the CLI package. Install the CLI package where you want\n  `itcmdr-cli` on PATH (admin PCs, or the server too). Both copies have the same version.\n- **Server and app on one PC:** install both packages. On a first install the server asks *This PC also runs the\n  itcmdr app*; tick it so first-run puts the admin key into the app's DPAPI store.\n- **Upgrading from the combined installer (v0.14.2 and older):** run the new package for each part you had (usually\n  server, agent and app) over the existing install. Files, `server.json`, data, keys, the service and signed-only mode\n  are kept. When the last of them is installed, the old combined \"itcmdr\" uninstall entry is removed; its files now\n  belong to the new packages. Until then, don't run the old uninstaller.\n- **Uninstall** (Apps & features, or `C:\\Program Files\\itcmdr\\uninstall\\<part>\\unins000.exe`):\n  - **Data is kept by default.** The uninstaller asks whether to keep this part's data (Yes is the default).\n  - If you choose to remove it, the server uninstaller asks separately about `keys\\` (No is the default). Without\n    that folder a restore needs the 24 words.\n  - Silent: `/VERYSILENT` keeps everything. `/REMOVEDATA=yes` removes the part's data, and for the server\n    `/REMOVEKEYS=yes` also removes `keys\\`.\n  - **Backups (`backups\\` and the backup folder) are never removed.** The words are never stored, so there's\n    nothing of them to remove.\n  - The app's key in `%APPDATA%\\itcmdr` and the agent's local log folder (`C:\\maintenanceRuns`) are always kept.\n\n## Server (Windows)\n\n1. Run `itcmdr-server-Setup-<ver>.exe` from the Releases page as an admin, at the server. Then run the app and agent packages if this PC needs them.\n   The server package adds:\n   - the `itcmdr-server` service (`itcmdr-server.exe --service`, delayed auto-start, restart on failure)\n   - `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\server.json`, which is never overwritten\n   - an inbound firewall rule for TCP 50051, limited to `/AllowSubnet=<lan cidr>`\n   - the `itcmdr Log Rotation` scheduled task (daily, SYSTEM, `rotation.timeLocal`, default 02:10)\n2. On a first install the wizard asks for the **site settings**: site name, server name (for example SERVER01), the\n   maintenanceRuns share (for example `\\\\SERVER01\\DATA\\maintenanceRuns`, or its local folder on the server), and\n   the backup folder (a share or a second disk). It also asks for the **first admin** (a person's name). These go into\n   `server.json` (`itcmdr-cli setup apply`). The agent package asks for its own values, which go into `site.json` (`Initialize-ItcmdrSite.ps1`). An upgrade keeps every value\n   that is already set and only fills empty ones.\n\n   **No file share is needed (v1.0.0).** Leave the maintenanceRuns and backup fields empty and the server keeps\n   logs in `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\maintenanceRuns` and backups in `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\backups` (set a second\n   disk later with `itcmdr-cli setup apply --backup-dir`). Install the agent with only `/EnrollToken=<token>`\n   (`itcmdr-cli agent enroll-token --machine <PC> --address <server>:50052`): it reports, takes config and Run now over\n   its mutual-TLS link. Tested on the Windows VM in v1.0.0 (fresh install, no share: enrolled, online, backup and\n   restore drill OK, no share path anywhere in the config).\n3. Have paper ready, leave **Run first-run setup now** ticked, and click Finish. A console window opens\n   (`itcmdr-cli setup first-run`):\n   - It shows the 24 recovery words once. They are never written to a file or a log. Write them on paper and keep the\n     paper off the server (for example in the office safe). They are the only way to certify keys and to open a backup.\n   - Press Enter, then type the three words it asks for. On a mismatch it shows the words again.\n   - It registers the first admin's app key (role owner). With *This PC also runs the itcmdr app* ticked, the key is created in your Windows\n     DPAPI key store, so the itcmdr app on this PC signs from the first start. Signed mode works from day one, and the\n     shared token is never needed.\n   - The words are only shown in a real console. Redirected output is refused.\n4. Skipped it, or a silent install? Run it later, in person, in an admin console:\n   `& 'C:\\Program Files\\itcmdr\\server\\itcmdr-cli.exe' setup first-run --admin-name \"Dr Example\" --app-key-store`\n   (or `--admin-public-key <hex>` from the Keys screen of an app on another PC).\n5. Check that it's working:\n   - `itcmdr-cli setup check` (also saved by the installer to `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\setup-check.txt`). It checks\n     that the config, data dir, log share and backup folder exist and are writable, that the server is initialized,\n     and that an admin app key exists. Exit code 1 when a check fails.\n   - `Get-Service itcmdr-server` shows Running.\n   - Errors, if any, are in `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\server-logs\\itcmdr-server.txt`.\n\nSilent install (values as parameters; never shows the words):\n`itcmdr-server-Setup-<ver>.exe /VERYSILENT /LogsDir=\"D:\\DATA\\maintenanceRuns\" /BackupDir=\"\\\\NAS01\\backup\\itcmdr\" /AllowSubnet=198.51.100.0/24`, then step 4.\n\nThe shared admin token (`admin_token_hash`, `itcmdr-server.exe hash-token`) is only for `auth set-mode both` (older installs).\n\nConsole mode, for troubleshooting: stop the service, then run `itcmdr-server.exe --console`. It reads the same config, from `ITCMDR_SERVER_CONFIG`, `.\\config\\server.json`, `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr\\server.json`, or `server.json` next to the exe.\n\n## Agents\n\nSilent agent install on a PC (push-friendly; the enroll token is optional and kept for the Rust agent in v0.17.0):\n\n```\nitcmdr-agent-Setup-<ver>.exe /VERYSILENT /SUPPRESSMSGBOXES /NORESTART /SiteName=\"Example Office\" /ServerHostname=SERVER01 /Share=\"\\\\SERVER01\\DATA\\maintenanceRuns\" [/EnrollToken=<token>]\n```\n\nThis registers two tasks, both running as SYSTEM:\n- `itcmdr Agent Nightly` at 01:30\n- `itcmdr Agent Poller` every minute; a run that's still going makes the next one skip\n\nEach PC also needs `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr\\site.json`.\n\nManual install, or push to several PCs with `agent\\Push-Install-ItcmdrAgent.ps1` (uses ADMIN\$ and schtasks, no WinRM):\n\n```\nSet-ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Scope Process -Force\n.\\Install-ItcmdrAgent.ps1\n```\n\nTo do a one-off test run (report-only when not SYSTEM), run `.\\Invoke-ItcmdrAgent.ps1 -ReportOnly`. Then look at the newest file in `C:\\maintenanceRuns`.\n\n## App\n\n- Windows: `itcmdr-app-Setup-<ver>.exe` (Start menu shortcut; `/TASKS=desktopicon` for a desktop icon).\n- Web: not supported for the on-site app (its key store is Windows DPAPI). The web build comes with the adminsvr app.\n- Open **Settings** and enter the server host and port (default 50051) and your name for the audit log.\n- Open **Keys** and make or import a key. An owner certifies it (see the server steps above). The key seed stays in this Windows profile.\n- The shared admin token field is only for an install still in `both` or `token` mode.\n\n## Switch to signed-only (on site, after the app works signed)\n\nRun on SERVER01 in an admin PowerShell. Placeholders: SERVER01 (server), WS-01 (the PC with the app),\n`Dr Example` (the key name). Nothing here needs the 24 words except step 3.\n\n1. Check the starting point:\n   `& 'C:\\Program Files\\itcmdr\\server\\itcmdr-cli.exe' auth status`\n   Expect `auth_mode: both`, and note how many admin/owner keys can sign.\n2. On WS-01, open the app and go to Keys. Make a key, or keep the existing one. Check that it says\n   \"Private key stored in: Windows DPAPI (current user)\". Copy the certify command it shows.\n3. Certify the key offline with the words and register it on SERVER01:\n   `itcmdr-cli keys certify --kind user --name \"Dr Example\" --role owner --public-key <hex> --out C:\\Temp\\app.bundle.json`\n   (type the words when asked), then `itcmdr-cli keys register C:\\Temp\\app.bundle.json`, then delete the bundle file.\n   Alternatively, register the bundle from the Keys screen of an app that already has an owner key.\n4. On WS-01, check that the app works signed: Keys shows the name and role (WhoAmI), and Schedules and Plugins load.\n5. Make sure at least one admin/owner key can sign besides the root key (`auth status`). Without one,\n   `set-mode signed` refuses (`--force` overrides it; don't use it here).\n6. Switch: `itcmdr-cli auth set-mode signed`, then `sc.exe stop itcmdr-server`, then `sc.exe start itcmdr-server`.\n7. Verify:\n   - `itcmdr-cli auth status` shows `auth_mode: signed (set in server.json)`.\n   - The app on WS-01 still loads Keys, Schedules and Plugins.\n   - A shared-token call is refused with `Unauthenticated` (\"missing x-itcmdr-key\").\n   - The next agent poller run finishes with result 0 (`Get-ScheduledTaskInfo 'itcmdr Agent Poller'`).\n8. Rollback, if anything is wrong: `itcmdr-cli auth set-mode both`, then restart the service as in step 6.\n   Token calls work again; signed calls keep working.\n9. Record the date and the result in the handover.\n\n## Local demo (Linux or Windows dev box)\n\n```\ncargo run -p itcmdr-sample-data-gen -- --out-dir ./sample-maintenanceRuns --days 90\ncp config/server.example.json config/server.json   # set allow_insecure_no_auth or a token hash\ncargo run -p itcmdr-server\ncd app && flutter run -d linux   # Settings: localhost : 50051\n```\n\nEvery Windows installer run writes a log under `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr\\install-logs` (adminsvr: `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr-adminsvr\\install-logs`); a failed upgrade exits 7 or 8 and starts the services again. See docs/UPGRADES.md, Installer logs.\n",
  "keys": "# Keys, roles and signed requests\n\n## The master key and the 24 words\n\n`itcmdr-cli setup first-run` (or `init`) creates the site's master key and shows it **once** as 24\nwords, in a real console only (redirected output is refused). They are never written to a file or a\nlog. Write them on paper and keep it off the server. They are needed to certify keys, to restore on a\nreplacement machine (`recover-db-key`) and to open a backup.\n\n## Keys and roles\n\nEvery person, app and service plugin has an Ed25519 key certified with the words. Roles, highest\nfirst: **owner > admin > user > client**.\n\n    itcmdr-cli keys certify --kind user --name \"Dr Example\" --role owner --public-key <hex> --out app.bundle.json\n    itcmdr-cli keys register app.bundle.json      # then delete the bundle file\n    itcmdr-cli keys list | revoke <fingerprint> | rotate ...\n    itcmdr-cli user add --name \"Front Desk\" --role user --public-key <hex>\n    itcmdr-cli service register ...               # a service plugin's client key\n\nPrefer `--public-key` from the app's Keys page: the private key never leaves the user's device.\n\n## Signed requests\n\nEach call carries `x-itcmdr-key`, `x-itcmdr-ts`, `x-itcmdr-nonce` and `x-itcmdr-sig` over the canonical\nrequest bytes ([signing](SIGNING.md)). The server checks the registered key, a clock window, a nonce\ncache and the role. New installs are signed-only (`auth_mode: signed`); older installs may still be in\n`both` (the legacy shared token for changes): `itcmdr-cli auth status`, then the switch steps in\n[install](INSTALL.md).\n\n## Agents\n\nAgents do not use the words: they enroll with a one-time token and get a client certificate from the\nsite CA, which is signed by the root key ([agent](AGENT.md)).\n\n## adminsvr\n\nadminsvr devices have their own 24 words per user and device keys; see [adminsvr](ADMINSVR.md) and\n[admin CLI](ADMIN-CLI.md).\n",
  "log-standard": "# itcmdr.log/v1: structured plugin logs (decision 11)\n\nEvery service plugin writes **JSON Lines**: one JSON object per line, UTF8 text, `\\n` line ends. The\nserver validates each line, counts the bad ones and turns `metrics` into chart data. The same rules\napply to the built-in checks when they become plugins (v0.11.0).\n\n## Where the log goes\n\n- Each run gets a log path from the server: `RunContext.log_path`, also in the environment as\n  `ITCMDR_LOG_PATH`.\n- The path is `<data>/plugin-logs/<plugin>/<yyyy-MM-dd>.jsonl` (local date). One file per plugin per\n  day; runs append, and each line carries its `run_id`.\n- The server writes a `run-start` and a `run-end` line around every run, so even a plugin that logs\n  nothing leaves a record.\n- After the run the day file is copied to the share at `<maintenanceRuns>/plugin-logs/<plugin>/`.\n  The share rotation (`Rotate-ItcmdrLogs.ps1`) puts finished weeks into the weekly zip as\n  `plugin-logs/<plugin>/<day>.jsonl`, so they also go into the 4-week blocks and yearly archives.\n- Server backups include `plugin-logs/**`. A restore merges them back without overwriting newer\n  files.\n\n## Fields\n\nRequired. Every one must be a string:\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `schema` | Always `itcmdr.log/v1` |\n| `ts` | RFC 3339 time **with an offset**, e.g. `2026-10-30T02:00:05.123-04:00` |\n| `level` | `debug`, `info`, `warn` or `error` |\n| `plugin` | Plugin name, as in `package.toml` |\n| `plugin_version` | Plugin version |\n| `run_id` | From `RunContext.run_id` |\n| `machine` | Machine the work is about, e.g. `SERVER01` |\n| `stage` | `verify`, `execute`, `log` (`start` / `end` on the server's run-start / run-end lines) |\n| `event` | Short machine-readable name: `check`, `metrics`, `stage-failed`, ... |\n| `message` | Text for people (may be empty) |\n\nOptional:\n\n| Field | Type | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `check_id` | string | e.g. `DISK-C` |\n| `status` | string | `ok`, `fail`, `repaired`, `skipped` or `excepted` |\n| `duration_ms` | integer | |\n| `metrics` | list | See below |\n| `criticality` | string | v0.15.0: `critical`, `cautious` or `informational` (required on `plugin-error` lines) |\n| `error` | string | v0.15.0: error message (max 4 KiB) |\n| `backtrace` | string | v0.15.0: backtrace (max 16 KiB) |\n| `kind` | string | v0.15.0: error type (struct name, PowerShell exception type) |\n| `context` | object | v0.15.0: extra fields of the error (string values) |\n\nOther fields are allowed and ignored, so readers older than v0.15.0 keep working.\n\n## Plugin errors (v0.15.0)\n\nA plugin error is written by the **server** (not the plugin) as one line with `event: \"plugin-error\"`,\n`message` = the error, and `level` from the criticality: critical \u2192 `error`, cautious \u2192 `warn`,\ninformational \u2192 `info`. Plugins raise them like this:\n\n- Rust: `#[derive(ServicePluginError)]` from `itcmdr-common` (fields `error`, `criticality`,\n  `backtrace`, `service_plugin`; extra fields go into `context`), then\n  `Ok(itcmdr_plugin::plugin_error(&e))` from a stage.\n- PowerShell: `throw (New-ItcmdrPluginError -Message ... -Criticality Critical|Cautious|Informational)`\n  fails the stage; `Write-ItcmdrPluginError` records a non-fatal one (the worst of the stage is sent).\n  An uncaught exception becomes a plugin error at the package's `default_error_criticality`\n  (`package.toml`, default `cautious`), with `\$_.ScriptStackTrace` and the position as backtrace.\n- A stage that fails without one (a plain error, a timeout) also gets a plugin-error line at the\n  package default, `kind` `stage-failed` or `timeout`.\n\n```json\n{\"schema\":\"itcmdr.log/v1\",\"ts\":\"2026-10-30T02:00:07.001-04:00\",\"level\":\"error\",\"plugin\":\"disk-free\",\"plugin_version\":\"1.2.0\",\"run_id\":\"7f1c\",\"machine\":\"SERVER01\",\"stage\":\"execute\",\"event\":\"plugin-error\",\"message\":\"backup share is gone\",\"criticality\":\"critical\",\"error\":\"backup share is gone\",\"backtrace\":\"at <ScriptBlock>, main.ps1: line 12\",\"kind\":\"ShareMissing\",\"context\":{\"share\":\"maintenanceRuns\"}}\n```\n\n## Call lines (v0.15.0)\n\nThe server exports its call log (one row per RPC) once a day to `<data>/server-logs/calls/calls-<date>.jsonl`\nas lines with `plugin: \"itcmdr-server\"`, `stage` and `event` `call`, `run_id` = the call id, `message`\n= `<method> <status>`, `duration_ms`, and `service`, `method`, `kind`, `peer`, `principal`, `grpc_status`,\n`req_bytes`, `resp_bytes`, `error`. `level` is `info` for OK, `warn` for client-side codes (auth,\nnot found, invalid argument, cancelled), `error` otherwise.\n\n## Metrics\n\n```json\n{\"name\": \"disk_free\", \"value\": 42.5, \"unit\": \"GiB\", \"kind\": \"gauge\", \"labels\": {\"drive\": \"C:\"}}\n```\n\n- `name`: required, not empty.\n- `value`: required, a number.\n- `kind`: required. `gauge` is a level, `counter` is a count since the last line, `histogram` is one\n  observation.\n- `unit`: optional.\n- `labels`: optional. All values must be strings.\n\n`GetPluginMetrics` returns the raw points, or daily values when you ask for them. Daily values sum\ncounters and average gauges and histograms. You can filter by name and labels.\n\n## Bad lines\n\nA line that is not JSON, or that breaks a rule above, is a **reject**. It is counted for its file\n(`plugin_log_files.rejects`) and reported by `GetPluginMetrics` (`rejects`). It never stops the\ningest, and the line's metrics are not used. Each line is read once: the server remembers how far it\ngot in each file.\n\n## Example\n\n```json\n{\"schema\":\"itcmdr.log/v1\",\"ts\":\"2026-10-30T02:00:05.120-04:00\",\"level\":\"info\",\"plugin\":\"disk-free\",\"plugin_version\":\"1.0.0\",\"run_id\":\"5c3e...\",\"machine\":\"SERVER01\",\"stage\":\"execute\",\"event\":\"check\",\"message\":\"C: has room\",\"check_id\":\"DISK-C\",\"status\":\"ok\",\"duration_ms\":12}\n{\"schema\":\"itcmdr.log/v1\",\"ts\":\"2026-10-30T02:00:05.200-04:00\",\"level\":\"info\",\"plugin\":\"disk-free\",\"plugin_version\":\"1.0.0\",\"run_id\":\"5c3e...\",\"machine\":\"SERVER01\",\"stage\":\"execute\",\"event\":\"metrics\",\"message\":\"disk_free = 42.5\",\"metrics\":[{\"name\":\"disk_free\",\"value\":42.5,\"unit\":\"GiB\",\"kind\":\"gauge\",\"labels\":{\"drive\":\"C:\"}}]}\n```\n\n## Helpers\n\n**PowerShell** (`agent/Itcmdr.Plugin.psm1`, Windows PowerShell 5.1 and 7):\n\n```powershell\nImport-Module \$env:ITCMDR_PS_MODULE\nInvoke-ItcmdrPlugin -Info @{ name = 'disk-free'; version = '1.0.0'; features = @('verify', 'execute', 'log') } `\n  -Verify  { param(\$Ctx) @{ ok = \$true; message = 'ready' } } `\n  -Execute { param(\$Ctx)\n             Write-ItcmdrLog -EventName 'check' -Message 'C: has room' -CheckId 'DISK-C' -Status 'ok'\n             Write-ItcmdrMetric -Name 'disk_free' -Value 42.5 -Unit 'GiB' -Labels @{ drive = 'C:' }\n             @{ ok = \$true; message = 'done'; results = @(@{ check_id = 'DISK-C'; status = 'ok'; message = '42.5 GiB' }) } } `\n  -Log     { param(\$Ctx, \$Stage, \$Err) Write-ItcmdrLog -Level 'error' -EventName 'stage-failed' -Message \"\${Stage}: \$Err\" }\n```\n\nAlso available: `New-ItcmdrLogRecord`, `New-ItcmdrMetric` and `Get-ItcmdrLogTimestamp`.\n\n**Rust** (`itcmdr-plugin` crate, `itcmdr_plugin::log`):\n\n```rust\nlet log = Logger::for_ctx(\"disk-free\", \"1.0.0\", &ctx);\nlog.check(\"DISK-C\", \"ok\", \"C: has room\", Some(12))?;\nlog.metric(Metric::gauge(\"disk_free\", 42.5, \"GiB\").label(\"drive\", \"C:\"))?;\n```\n\n`itcmdr_plugin::log::validate(line)` is the validator the server uses.\n\n## Plugin protocol (summary)\n\n- A plugin is a separate process. It serves `itcmdr.plugin.PluginService` (`proto/plugin.proto`) on\n  loopback and prints `ITCMDR-PLUGIN|1|tcp|127.0.0.1:<port>` on stdout.\n- The server passes a per-launch secret in `ITCMDR_PLUGIN_SECRET`. Every call carries it in\n  `x-itcmdr-plugin-secret`.\n- The order is `Describe` (the name must match), then `Verify` (if the plugin lists the feature),\n  then `Execute`.\n- A failed `Verify` stops the run before `Execute`. `Log` is called on any failure, with the\n  failed stage and the error.\n- Every call times out after `plugins.timeout_secs`.\n- The plugin exits when its stdin closes.\n- PowerShell plugins (`type = \"powershell\"` main file) run through `itcmdr-plugin-ps`, which calls the\n  script once per stage. The shell is the `plugins.powershell` setting (`powershell.exe` on Windows).\n",
  "man": "# Man pages\n\n`man/` holds one page per program (`itcmdr-cli.1`, `itcmdr-admin.1`, `itcmdr-adminsvr.1`,\n`itcmdr-agent.1`, both watchdogs, `itcmdr-server.1`, `itcmdr-restore.1`, `itcmdr-plugin-ps.1`) and\n`itcmdr.7` with every environment variable. They list every command, subcommand and flag.\n\nThey are generated, never edited by hand:\n\n    cargo build --workspace --bins\n    python3 scripts/gen-man.py            # writes man/\n    python3 scripts/gen-man.py --check    # exit 1 when man/ is out of date (the test suite runs it)\n\nThe flags come from each program's `--help`; the ENVIRONMENT sections come from\n[environment](ENVIRONMENT.md). Read one without installing: `man ./man/itcmdr-cli.1`.\n",
  "plugins": "# Plugins\n\nChecks and repairs come as signed plugin packages. The built-in checks are plugins too (pinned in\n`plugins.pin.json`, fetched by `scripts/fetch-plugins.sh`). A plugin is PowerShell (run by\n`itcmdr-plugin-ps`) or a Rust binary (the `itcmdr-plugin` crate), and speaks the plugin protocol on\nstdio with a per-launch secret ([log standard](LOG-STANDARD.md) has the protocol, the log format and\nthe error contract).\n\n## On the site server\n\n    itcmdr-cli plugins install --path disk-watch-1.2.0.tar.gz   # or --url; --disabled to install off\n    itcmdr-cli plugins list\n    itcmdr-cli plugins enable disk-watch[@1.2.0] | disable disk-watch | remove disk-watch[@1.2.0]\n    itcmdr-cli plugins run disk-watch                           # Verify, then Execute\n    itcmdr-cli plugins runs\n    itcmdr-cli plugins quarantine                               # refused packages\n    itcmdr-cli plugins pack --dir ./disk-watch --key owner.key --out disk-watch-1.2.0.tar.gz\n\n- Only owner-signed packages install; a bad signature or checksum is quarantined\n  (`quarantine/index.jsonl`) and the install is refused.\n- `plugins.enable_on_install` (default true) enables a valid package on install.\n\n## On the agents\n\nThe server pushes the plugin selection `agent.plugins` (endorsed by the root key) with file checksums;\nthe agent checks them and refuses tampered copies (alert, then resolved when fixed).\n\n    itcmdr-cli agent run WS-01 <plugin>     # report-only, now\n    itcmdr-cli agent runs                   # reports, with parity against the nightly\n    itcmdr-cli agent plugins                # what each agent applied\n\nThe site app's Plugins page and, from adminsvr, `itcmdr-admin plugin ...` (with the organization's\napproval rules) do the same.\n\nPlugin environment (set by the agent or server for each run): `ITCMDR_PLUGIN_SECRET`,\n`ITCMDR_LOG_PATH`, `ITCMDR_RUN_ID`, `ITCMDR_MACHINE`, `ITCMDR_REPORT_ONLY`, `ITCMDR_ARTIFACT_DIR`,\n`ITCMDR_PS_MODULE` ([environment](ENVIRONMENT.md)). A plugin has `ITCMDR_PLUGIN_START_SECS`\n(default 60) to answer the handshake.\n",
  "index": "# itcmdr documentation\n\nitcmdr is self-healing IT for small offices: an agent on every PC checks the network, connections and\nservices and repairs what it safely can; a site server in the office keeps the history, alerts and\nencrypted backups; nothing internal leaves the office. These pages are also the in-app help (the Help\npage in both apps, `itcmdr-cli help <topic>`, `itcmdr-admin help <topic>`).\n\n## Getting started\n\n- [Install](INSTALL.md): packages, the server, agents, the app, switching to signed-only\n- [Deploying a site server](DEPLOY-SITE-SERVER.md): the checklist for one office\n- [Architecture](ARCHITECTURE.md): components, runs, incidents, metrics, rollups\n\n## In the office\n\n- [Site server](SERVER.md): ports, configuration, what it does\n- [Site app](SITE-APP.md): every page\n- [itcmdr-cli](CLI.md): administration on the server\n- [Agent](AGENT.md): enrollment, the link, heartbeats, certificates, the offline queue\n- [Settings](SETTINGS.md): every runtime setting and where it comes from\n- [Alerts](ALERTS.md): levels, sources, dedupe, silences\n- [Schedules](SCHEDULES.md): scheduled runs, guards, the cutover from the nightly task\n- [Plugins](PLUGINS.md): signed packages, install, quarantine, runs on the agents\n- [Log standard](LOG-STANDARD.md): itcmdr.log/v1, plugin errors, the plugin protocol\n- [Backup and restore](BACKUP.md): nightly backups, the restore drill, restoring onto a new machine\n- [Upgrades and watchdogs](UPGRADES.md): the update window, agent rings, server upgrades, rollback\n\n## Security\n\n- [Keys, roles and signed requests](KEYS.md): the 24 words, certified keys, roles\n- [Signing](SIGNING.md): the canonical request encoding, sealed words, where keys are kept\n- [HTTPS and certificate trust](CERTIFICATES.md): system trust store, trust on first use, plain HTTP opt-in\n\n## For IT providers (optional)\n\n- [adminsvr](ADMINSVR.md): what leaves a site, install, commands, approvals, releases, passkeys, push, Let's Encrypt\n- [Deploying adminsvr with Let's Encrypt](DEPLOY-ADMINSVR.md): DNS, firewall, ACME settings per install\n- [adminsvr backup and restore](BACKUP-ADMINSVR.md): adminsvr's own encrypted backups, the drill, restoring onto a new machine\n- [Admin app](ADMIN-APP.md) and [itcmdr-admin](ADMIN-CLI.md)\n\n## Reference\n\n- [Environment variables](ENVIRONMENT.md): every `ITCMDR_*` variable\n- [Man pages](MAN.md): generated from the programs, every flag\n- [In-app help](HELP.md): how these pages are built into the apps and CLIs\n\n## Development\n\n- [Local release builds](RELEASE-LOCAL.md) and [the website](DEPLOY-WEBSITE.md)\n- [Handover notes](HANDOVER.md): the state of work, how to test, the release procedure\n- [Changelog](../CHANGELOG.md) and [roadmap](../PLAN.md)\n- [1.0 archive](historical/1.0/): the finished plans, design documents and the changelog through 1.0\n",
  "release-local": "# Local release builds\n\nUsed while GitHub Actions is unavailable (v0.15.0 and later). Nothing here uses Actions minutes. The tag is created\nand pushed first by the normal release procedure (docs/HANDOVER.md); these steps build from that exact tag and never\nmove or recreate it.\n\n## Linux step (dev box)\n\n    git checkout vX.Y.Z            # or stay on main at the tagged commit\n    NINJA_DIR=<dir with ninja> scripts/release-local.sh unix\n\nProduces `dist/X.Y.Z/itcmdr-cli-X.Y.Z-linux-x86_64.tar.gz` and `itcmdr-app-X.Y.Z-linux-x86_64.tar.gz`. The Linux\napp build needs clang, cmake, pkg-config, GTK 3 headers and ninja; a user-local ninja binary is enough.\n\n## macOS step (Mac)\n\nClone the tag on the Mac and run `scripts/release-local.sh unix`, `android` and `ios-check` with a Flutter that\nmatches `app/pubspec.yaml`. Produces the CLI, admin CLI and adminsvr tarballs and the app and admin app zips\n(unsigned, not notarized until the Mac signing step), plus the admin app APK. Copy the files into `dist/X.Y.Z/`\non the dev box.\n\nNo CocoaPods (v0.20.0): iOS and macOS use Flutter's Swift Package Manager integration and the Rust bridges are\nbuilt by flutter_rust_bridge native assets (`hook/build.dart`, toolchain pinned in `rust/rust-toolchain.toml`,\ninstalled by rustup). Once per user: `flutter config --enable-swift-package-manager` (the script turns it on if\noff). The script refuses to build when a Podfile or Pods folder exists. All plugins in both apps support this\n(the only native code is the bridge; `path_provider` and `objective_c` need no pods).\n\n## Windows VM step\n\n1. `git archive --format=zip -o <share>/src-rX.zip vX.Y.Z` plus `third_party/` (as `sync.sh` does).\n2. On the VM: expand it, `cargo build --release --locked -p itcmdr-server -p itcmdr-agent --bins`, `flutter build windows --release`,\n   then `ISCC itcmdr-{server,agent,cli,app}.iss`.\n3. Copy `installer\\out\\*.exe` back into `dist/X.Y.Z/`.\n4. Scan the uncompressed payload (release exes, the Flutter runner folder, agent and installer files) for the\n   private leak patterns; the installers themselves are compressed.\n\nSigning: pass `/DSign` with signtool and the code-signing certificate when one exists (see release.yml). Until then\nthe installers are unsigned and the release notes say so.\n\n## Check and publish (dev box)\n\n    ITCMDR_LEAK_PATTERNS=<private extra patterns file> scripts/release-local.sh check\n    scripts/release-local.sh publish\n\n`check` unpacks every artifact, greps it for `config/banned.local.txt` plus the extra patterns (VM and machine\nhost names), refuses on any hit, then writes `SHA256SUMS`. `publish` needs the tag on origin and creates the GitHub\nrelease with the CHANGELOG section as notes (`--verify-tag`), or uploads to an existing release.\n\n## Admin app desktop builds (v0.22.0)\n\n- macOS: `release-local.sh unix` on the Mac builds `itcmdr-admin-app-<v>-macos-aarch64.zip` with\n  Swift Package Manager (`flutter config --enable-swift-package-manager`; there is no Podfile and\n  the script refuses to call `pod`). Unsigned and not notarized.\n- Windows: built on the Windows VM (`flutter build windows` in `admin-app`, then Inno Setup) as\n  `itcmdr-admin-app-Setup-<v>.exe`. Unsigned.\n- Linux tarball and Android APK (debug-signed) come from the Linux and Mac runs.\n",
  "schedules": "# Schedules\n\nScheduled runs live on the site server and are fired by the agents (also while the server is\noffline; reports are delivered later). Two built-in schedules exist: `nightly-agent-run` and\n`nightly-backup`.\n\n    itcmdr-cli schedule add --name \"weekday morning\" --when \"weekdays 07:00\" --tz local --all --categories network\n    itcmdr-cli schedule list\n    itcmdr-cli schedule edit <id> --when \"daily 01:30\"\n    itcmdr-cli schedule pause <id> | resume <id> | run-now <id>\n    itcmdr-cli schedule history [<id>]\n    itcmdr-cli schedule reset-to-default nightly-agent-run\n    itcmdr-cli schedule rm <id>                  # built-ins: pause instead (rm needs --force --confirm <name>)\n\n- `--when`: `hourly`, `every 6h`, `daily 01:30`, `weekdays 07:00`, `weekly mon 02:00`, or `cron M H DOM MON DOW`;\n  `--tz` an IANA zone or `local`.\n- Targets: `--all`, `--machines`, or `--groups` (agent roles: Server, Workstation, RCU). `--plugins` runs\n  service plugins on the server.\n- Report-only by default; `--repair` allows repairs. `--guard report-only-in-hours` (default),\n  `skip-in-hours` or `none` protects office hours (`agent.officeHours`). The agent's clinical guard\n  (recent changes to clinical data make the run report-only) always applies, also to `run-now`.\n- Missed runs: `--missed skip` (default) or `catch-up`.\n\n## Cutover from the nightly task\n\nOlder installs ran the nightly from a Windows scheduled task. `itcmdr-cli cutover status` shows\nthe parity results and what still blocks the switch; `cutover switch --to agent` switches only when\nevery gate passes; `cutover rollback` puts the task back in one command.\n",
  "server": "# Site server (`itcmdr-server`)\n\nThe site server runs on one machine in the office (a Windows service, or `--console` for\ntroubleshooting). It keeps the history, alerts, metrics, schedules, plugins, keys and encrypted\nbackups, and it is the only part that decides what may leave the office.\n\n## Ports\n\n| Port | Setting (server.json) | What | Who connects |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 50051 | `bind_addr` | HTTPS API (gRPC and gRPC-Web) for the site app and `itcmdr-cli` | admin PCs in the office |\n| 50052 | `agent_bind_addr` | agent link: TLS with client certificates (mTLS), pinned | every enrolled agent |\n\nThe Windows installer binds both to all addresses and adds firewall rules limited to\n`/AllowSubnet=<lan cidr>`. A server.json without `bind_addr` listens on `127.0.0.1:50051` only.\n`agent_bind_addr: \"\"` turns the agent link off.\n\nSince v1.1.0 the API is HTTPS with the server certificate the agents already pin\n(`<data dir>\\keys\\agent-server.pem`). Plain HTTP is accepted from the same machine only, or from\nthe network with `api_plain_http: true` (see [certificates](CERTIFICATES.md)).\n\n## Configuration\n\n- `server.json` is read from `ITCMDR_SERVER_CONFIG`, `.\\config\\server.json`,\n  `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr\\server.json`, or `server.json` next to the exe, in that order. The installer\n  writes it once and never overwrites it (`itcmdr-cli setup apply --keep-existing` only fills empty values).\n- The data folder (`--data-dir`, `ITCMDR_DATA_DIR`, then `data_dir` in server.json; default\n  `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr`) holds `itcmdr.database` (encrypted), `keys\\`, `service_plugins\\`,\n  `quarantine\\`, `backups\\` and `server-logs\\`.\n- Runtime settings live in the database and override server.json: see [settings](SETTINGS.md).\n\n## Running it\n\n    sc.exe start itcmdr-server                 # the service (itcmdr-server.exe --service)\n    itcmdr-server.exe --console                # foreground, same config (stop the service first)\n    itcmdr-server.exe init | recover-db-key    # same as itcmdr-cli init / recover-db-key\n    itcmdr-server.exe hash-token <token>       # legacy shared token hash (auth mode \"both\" only)\n\n`itcmdr-server-watchdog` keeps the service running (restart when its health file goes stale) and\nswaps in server upgrades with a rollback ([upgrades](UPGRADES.md)). Errors are in\n`%ProgramData%\\itcmdr\\server-logs\\itcmdr-server.txt`.\n\n## What it does\n\n- Ingests agent reports (over the agent link) and, for older installs, JSON Lines logs from `logs_dir`;\n  tracks incidents per machine and check, computes [metrics](ARCHITECTURE.md) and rollups.\n- Raises and dedupes [alerts](ALERTS.md); runs [schedules](SCHEDULES.md) and [plugins](PLUGINS.md).\n- Nightly encrypted [backups](BACKUP.md) and the restore drill; restores through `itcmdr-restore`.\n- Signed requests and roles ([keys](KEYS.md), [signing](SIGNING.md)); a hash-chained audit log; a call log\n  with one row per RPC (`itcmdr-cli calls list|show|prune`, the app's Calls page; `calllog.retention_days`).\n- The optional offsite link to [adminsvr](ADMINSVR.md), deny by default.\n\nAdministration on the server itself is [itcmdr-cli](CLI.md). Installing: [install](INSTALL.md) and\n[deploying a site server](DEPLOY-SITE-SERVER.md).\n",
  "settings": "# Settings (decision 15)\n\nEvery tunable value in itcmdr is a typed setting with today's behaviour as its default. Source of truth: the registry in\n`server/src/settings.rs` (unit test `defaults_equal_current_behaviour` pins the defaults to the old hard-coded values).\n\n## Precedence\n\n- **Server settings:** DB override (RPC/app/CLI) > `server.json` > default. Read-only keys come from `server.json` only.\n- **Agent settings:** local `site.json` (same dotted name: `agent.quietMinutes` = `{\"agent\":{\"quietMinutes\":5}}`) >\n  central per-machine override > central global value > default. The server writes the central values to\n  `<maintenanceRuns share>\\agent-settings.json` (schema `itcmdr-agent-settings/v1`) at startup and after every change;\n  agents cache it in `<paths.agentData>` so offline runs still use it.\n\n## Changing settings\n\n- gRPC `ListSettings(machine)` / `UpdateSetting(key, value, machine, unset)`: admin/owner only, validated server-side,\n  each change audit-logged as `settings-update` (key, value, machine). Invalid values return `INVALID_ARGUMENT`.\n- CLI: `itcmdr-cli config list [--machine PC]`, `config get KEY [--machine PC]`, `config set KEY VALUE [--machine PC]`,\n  `config unset KEY [--machine PC]`. `--machine` is accepted for agent settings only. CLI changes are audit-logged too.\n- App: Settings tab, *Server & agent settings*. The list can be searched by name or description. Bool settings are switches; other types use the editor. Read-only keys (`server.json` only) show a lock.\n- The older `GetSettings` / `UpdateSettings` (backup block) RPCs still work and use the same storage.\n\n## Reference\n\n| Name | Scope | Type / range | Default | Applies | Who can change | Description |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `adminsvr.enabled` | server | bool | `true` | next pass | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Kill switch for the adminsvr link: false closes it and nothing from adminsvr is accepted. Only matters once linked (`itcmdr-cli adminsvr enroll`) (v0.19.0). |\n| `adminsvr.heartbeatSecs` | server | int 0..3600 | `0` | next link | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Seconds between status messages to adminsvr; 0 = adminsvr's value from enrollment (default 60) (v0.19.0). |\n| `adminsvr.requireTwoPerson` | server | bool | `false` | next command | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI); never remotely | Always require a second owner or admin's signature on plugin commands and release approvals from adminsvr, whatever the organization's setting says. Default off since v0.21.0: the site follows the organization's signed setting (v0.20.0). |\n| `agent.backupMaxAgeHours` | agent | int 1..720 | `26` | next run | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Default max age of a backup task run / newest backup file (per-item maxAgeHours wins). |\n| `agent.backupMinBytes` | agent | int 0..1_000_000_000_000 | `1` | next run | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Default minimum size of the newest backup file (per-item minBytes wins). |\n| `agent.nightlyTime` | agent | time | `01:30` | agent reinstall | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Nightly maintenance task time (before stagger); also the default time of the built-in nightly-agent-run schedule. |\n| `agent.pluginMode` | agent | text: auto, off, parallel, plugins | `auto` | next run | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Built-in checks (v0.11.0): auto = plugin versions report-only in parallel before the cutover, plugins after it. |\n| `agent.fallbackGraceMinutes` | agent | int 15..720 | `120` | next poll | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | After the switch: run the nightly locally when no scheduled run arrived this long after agent.nightlyTime. |\n| `agent.serverStaleMinutes` | agent | int 2..1440 | `15` | next poll | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | schedule-state.json older than this means the server is unreachable (local fallback). |\n| `agent.printerStuckMinutes` | agent | int 5..10080 | `60` | next run | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | A print job older than this counts as stuck. |\n| `agent.quietMinutes` | agent | int 0..240 | `10` | next run | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Clinical guard: no user input for this many minutes before disruptive repairs. |\n| `agent.staleLockMinutes` | agent | int 10..1440 | `150` | next run | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Poller: a request lock older than this was left by a crashed run. |\n| `agent.heartbeatSecs` | agent | int 10..3600 | `60` | next link | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Rust agent heartbeat (ping) interval in seconds (v0.17.0). |\n| `agent.lateHeartbeats` | agent | int 1..1000 | `2` | next pass | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Missed heartbeats before an agent shows as late (no alert) (v0.17.0). |\n| `agent.missedHeartbeats` | agent | int 1..1000 | `5` | next pass | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Missed heartbeats before an agent is non-responsive and an alert is raised (v0.17.0). |\n| `agent.nonresponsiveLevel` | agent | text: critical, cautious, informational | `critical` | next pass | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Level of the non-responsive alert (v0.17.0). |\n| `agent.serverNonresponsiveLevel` | agent | text: critical, cautious, informational | `critical` | next pass | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Level of the non-responsive alert for agents enrolled as role server (v0.17.0). |\n| `agent.reconnectMaxSecs` | agent | int 5..3600 | `300` | next link | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Longest wait between agent reconnect attempts (backoff cap, seconds) (v0.17.0). |\n| `agent.officeHours` | agent | text (`Mon-Fri 07:00-18:00`, or empty) | (empty) | next pass | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Heartbeat office-hours profile in server local time: outside the hours a missing agent raises no alert; misses count from the start of the hours. Empty: always (v0.17.1). |\n| `agent.plugins` | agent | text (`all`, `none`, or `name1,name2`) | all | next link sync (30 s) | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Installed, enabled plugins pushed to the Rust agent; others are removed from it (v0.18.0). |\n| `agent.ring` | agent | int 0..2 | `2` | next offer | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Upgrade ring of this machine: 0 = test VM/PC (first), 1 = one PC, 2 = everything else (v0.21.0). |\n| `agent.upgrades` | agent | bool | `true` | next offer | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Automatic agent upgrades through the rings; false holds this machine (or every machine) back (v0.21.0). |\n| `agent.upgradeSoakMins` | agent | int 1..10080 | `60` | next tick | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Minutes a ring must stay healthy on the new agent version before the next ring starts (v0.21.0). |\n| `agent.upgradeHealthSecs` | agent | int 30..3600 | `300` | next offer | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | The upgraded agent must connect with the new version within this, or the watchdog rolls back (v0.21.0). |\n| `agent.queueMaxMib` | agent | int 1..4096 | `256` | next link | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Rust agent offline alert queue cap (MiB); oldest informational items are dropped first and the drop is reported (v0.17.1). |\n| `agent.certLifetimeMins` | server | int 10..2628000 | `129600` | next enroll/renewal | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Agent client certificate lifetime in minutes (90 days); agents renew over the link at 2/3 of it (v0.17.1). |\n| `agent.tcpTimeoutMs` | agent | int 100..60000 | `3000` | next run | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | TCP probe timeout (ms). |\n| `auth.max_skew_secs` | server | int 30..3600 | `300` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Allowed clock difference for signed requests (seconds). |\n| `backup.dir` | server | path (must exist, writable; empty allowed) | `(empty = <data_dir>/backups)` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Backup archive folder (e.g. the share). Empty = <data_dir>/backups. |\n| `backup.hour` | server | int 0..23 | `2` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Default time (HH:00) of the built-in nightly-backup schedule (v0.11.0; afterwards edit the schedule; reset-to-default re-reads it). |\n| `backup.keep_blocks` | server | int 0..1300 | `13` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Nightly backups kept: newest per 4-week block (ISO weeks 4N-3..4N). |\n| `backup.keep_daily` | server | int 0..3650 | `7` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Nightly backups kept: newest per day, for this many days. |\n| `backup.keep_weekly` | server | int 0..520 | `4` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Nightly backups kept: newest per ISO week, for this many weeks. |\n| `backup.nightly` | server | bool | `true` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Default of the built-in nightly-backup schedule: on (v0.11.0; afterwards pause/resume the schedule). |\n| `alerts.dedupe_window_mins` | server | int 0..10080 | `60` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | A resolved alert raised again within this many minutes reopens instead of starting a new one (v0.16.0). |\n| `calllog.retention_days` | server | int 1..3650 | `30` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Days of the call log (every RPC) kept in the database; finished days are exported to server-logs/calls first (v0.15.0). |\n| `metrics.flap_min` | server | int 2..1000 | `3` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | ok<->failed changes in the window before a check counts as flapping. |\n| `metrics.repeat_min` | server | int 2..1000 | `3` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Incidents in the window before a machine/check is a repeat offender. |\n| `paths.agentData` | agent | path (on each PC) | `C:\\ProgramData\\itcmdr` | next run | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Agent cache (exceptions, settings) and spool folder. |\n| `paths.localLogs` | agent | path (on each PC) | `C:\\maintenanceRuns` | next run | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Local run logs and requests\\ folder on each PC. |\n| `plugins.enable_on_install` | server | bool | `true` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Enable a package that passed validation when it is installed (other versions are disabled). |\n| `plugins.max_package_mib` | server | int 1..2048 | `256` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Largest plugin package accepted (MiB). |\n| `plugins.powershell` | server | text | `powershell.exe` (Windows; `pwsh` elsewhere) | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | PowerShell used by itcmdr-plugin-ps (Windows PowerShell 5.1 by default). |\n| `plugins.timeout_secs` | server | int 10..86400 | `600` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Per-stage timeout of a plugin run (seconds). |\n| `restore.health_timeout_secs` | server | int 5..3600 | `120` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | How long the restore helper waits for the restarted server before rolling back. |\n| `retention.agentLocalDays` | agent | int 1..3650 | `400` | next run | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Days of local run logs kept on each PC. |\n| `rotation.timeLocal` | agent | time | `02:10` | server reinstall | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Log rotation task time on the server. |\n| `schedule.grace_secs` | server | int 30..86400 | `300` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | A run due longer ago than this counts as missed (server was down). |\n| `schedule.office_hours` | server | text | `Mon-Fri 07:00-18:00` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Office hours for the schedule guard, in each schedule's timezone. |\n| `search.default_limit` | server | int 1..1000 | `100` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Log search page size when the client asks for none. |\n| `search.max_limit` | server | int 100..10000 | `1000` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Largest log search page. |\n| `server.auth_mode` | server | text | `signed` | restart | nobody at runtime (server.json + restart) | token / both / signed (server.json only: security-critical). Default `signed` since v0.12.0. An install from before v0.12.0 with no value keeps `both` (`itcmdr-cli auth status`). |\n| `server.bind_addr` | server | text | `127.0.0.1:50051` | restart | nobody at runtime (server.json + restart) | gRPC listen address (server.json only). |\n| `server.ingest_interval_secs` | server | int 5..3600 | `30` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Seconds between log ingest / request sync passes. |\n| `server.request_timeout_secs` | server | int 60..604800 | `10800` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | On-demand requests still queued/running after this many seconds become timed_out. |\n| `server.retention_days` | server | int 30..3650 | `400` | next pass / next use | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI) | Days of run/check data kept in the database. |\n| `updates.auto` | server | bool | `true` | next tick | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI); adminsvr | Install approved server and agent releases automatically inside the update window; false turns automatic updates off for this site (a push from adminsvr or `upgrade start --now` still installs) (v0.21.0). |\n| `updates.timezone` | server | text | `local` | next tick | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI); adminsvr | Zone of updates.window: an IANA name like America/New_York, or local = this server's zone (v0.21.0). |\n| `updates.window` | server | text | `daily 01:00-04:00` | next tick | admin/owner (RPC, app, CLI); adminsvr | When upgrades (the server, agent offers and ring advances) may start, in site time: \"daily 01:00-04:00\", \"Mon-Fri 22:00-02:00\" (a range ending before it starts runs past midnight and belongs to its start day) (v0.21.0). |\n\n## server.json only\n\n- `restore.stop_command` / `restore.start_command`: argv lists the restore helper runs to stop and\n  start the service (default `[\"sc.exe\", \"stop\", \"itcmdr-server\"]` / `[\"sc.exe\", \"start\", \"itcmdr-server\"]` on\n  Windows, empty elsewhere). They run commands, so they are not changeable over RPC.\n\n## Not settings (on purpose)\n\n- `keys/`, the DB file name and the data-dir layout: fixed so backup/restore and recovery stay predictable.\n- Format constants (`ITCMDRB1`, signature domains, SQLCipher parameters): part of the on-disk formats.\n- Where `site.json` and `server.json` are found (bootstrap).\n- Per-item values already in site.json (`backup.tasks[].maxAgeHours`, `destinations[].minBytes`, scanner ports, ...):\n  they override the matching `agent.*` defaults.\n- The installer's build-time `LocalLogs` define and the app's connection settings (stored on the device).\n",
  "signing": "# Signed requests: canonical encoding\n\nSince v0.14.2 the server, the CLIs and the app all sign with the same code, the `itcmdr-keys` crate (`keys/`). The app reaches it through flutter_rust_bridge (`app/rust`).\n\nEvery signed gRPC call carries `x-itcmdr-key` (key fingerprint), `x-itcmdr-ts` (unix ms),\n`x-itcmdr-nonce` (16-128 chars of `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, never reused) and `x-itcmdr-sig` (hex Ed25519).\nThe signature covers:\n\n```\nitcmdr-req/v1\\n{gRPC path}\\n{ts}\\n{nonce}\\n{sha256 hex of the request body}\n```\n\nThe server does not see the client's raw bytes: it hashes its own protobuf **re-encoding** of the\ndecoded request. A client therefore has to produce the canonical encoding, which prost (Rust) and the\nofficial Dart `protobuf` package both do by default:\n\n1. Fields in ascending field-number order.\n2. Proto3 default values are omitted: `0`, `false`, `\"\"`, empty repeated fields, unset messages.\n   A set sub-message is written even when all its fields are default.\n3. Repeated scalars (none in the API today) are packed; repeated strings are one entry per item.\n4. Strings are UTF8 bytes, not Unicode-normalized; no unknown fields; no duplicate fields; minimal varints.\n5. Negative `int32`/`int64` are 10-byte two's-complement varints (not zigzag).\n\nPinned vectors: `proto/signing_vectors.json` (body hex plus the exact signed message for a fixed ts and\nnonce). `server/tests/signing.rs` and `app/test/signing_test.dart` both match them byte for byte.\nNever change a vector to make a test pass: a difference means clients and server disagree.\n\n## Sealed restore words\n\nAn app-triggered restore never sends the 24 words in plaintext. `GetRestoreKey` returns a one-time\nX25519 public key that lives only in the server's memory (10 minutes, bound to the caller's signing\nkey). The app seals the words (`app/lib/signing.dart` `sealWords`, `server/src/restore_seal.rs`):\n\n```\nkey = HKDF (SHA-256)(salt = eph_pub || server_pub, ikm = X25519(eph, server), info = \"itcmdr/restore-words/v1\")\naad = \"itcmdr-restore-words/v1\\n{key_id}\\n{backup}\"\nct  = XChaCha20-Poly1305(key, 24-byte nonce, words encoded as UTF8, aad)\n```\n\n`StageRestore` requires `sealed_words`. A dry run must pass first; the real restore then sends that\n`dry_run_id` (30 minutes, same signing key and same backup). Plaintext `words` are refused. These\ncalls, and the key RPCs, accept only a signed request. Pinned bytes: `proto/app_vectors.json`.\n\n## Where the app keeps its private key\n\nThe app's Ed25519 seed is never stored in the app preferences (from v0.13.0). It is kept in:\n\n| OS | Store | Encrypted |\n|---|---|---|\n| Windows | DPAPI, current user (`%APPDATA%\\itcmdr\\app-key.dpapi`, entropy `itcmdr-app-key/v1`). Only the same Windows user on the same PC can decrypt it. | yes |\n| Linux | Secret Service keyring via `secret-tool` (attributes `service=itcmdr`, `account=app-key`) when `secret-tool` is installed and the keyring is unlocked | yes |\n| Linux fallback | `\$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/itcmdr/app-key` (default `~/.config`), mode 0600. Used when there is no working keyring. Protected only by the file mode and disk encryption. The Keys screen warns about it. | no |\n| macOS | login Keychain via `security` (service `itcmdr`, account `app-key`) | yes |\n\nMigration: on first start, a key found in the old `auth.key_seed` preference is written to the store and read back. Only then is the preference overwritten with zeros and removed. If the read-back fails, the preference is kept and the app keeps working. A plaintext key file that is replaced is overwritten with random bytes before it is deleted.\n\nA DPAPI key does not move with a Windows profile copied to another PC. On a new PC, make a new key (Keys screen) and register it, then revoke the old one.\n",
  "site-app": "# Site app (itcmdr)\n\nThe office app (Flutter; Windows, macOS, Linux) talks to the site server's HTTPS API. Every request\nis signed with your app key; the key seed stays in your user profile (Windows DPAPI on Windows).\n\n## App id (v1.2.0)\n\nThe app's id is `com.weirdbrains.itcmdr` (Linux application id, macOS bundle id; Windows company\n`com.weirdbrains`). Before 1.2.0 it was the Flutter placeholder `com.example.app`. Upgrading keeps\nworking in place on every platform (there is no Android or iOS build of the office app):\n\n- Windows and Linux: the settings (server, HTTPS, trusted certificates) are copied once from the\n  old folder (`%APPDATA%\\com.example\\itcmdr`, `~/.local/share/com.example.app`) on first start.\n- macOS: the settings live in the app's sandbox container, which is per bundle id, so enter the\n  server and trust its certificate once more after upgrading. The app key is in the keychain and\n  is kept.\n\n## First start\n\n1. **Settings**: the server host and port (default 50051), your name for the audit log, and\n   **Use HTTPS** (on by default; plain HTTP asks first). The first connection shows the server's\n   certificate fingerprint: compare it with the server and trust it ([certificates](CERTIFICATES.md)).\n2. **Keys**: make or import a key and have an owner certify it ([keys](KEYS.md)). With\n   *This PC also runs the itcmdr app* ticked in the server installer, first-run setup does this for you.\n\n## Pages\n\n| Page | What it shows or does |\n|---|---|\n| Overview | machines, open incidents, recent runs; refreshes live |\n| Alerts | open alerts worst first (the badge counts them); acknowledge, resolve, silence ([alerts](ALERTS.md)) |\n| Agents | enrolled agents and their heartbeat state, enrollment tokens, maintenance, revoke ([agent](AGENT.md)) |\n| Metrics | incidents, uptime, repair time and failures over 7, 30, 90 or 365 days, with charts |\n| Logs | search runs and check results |\n| Exceptions | allowlist rules (ignore or downgrade a check, with an expiry) |\n| On-demand | run checks now on some or all machines |\n| Schedules | scheduled runs: add, edit, pause, run now, history ([schedules](SCHEDULES.md)) |\n| Plugins | installed plugins, enable, disable, quarantine ([plugins](PLUGINS.md)) |\n| Updates | the update window and agent/server rollouts ([upgrades](UPGRADES.md)) |\n| Backup | backups, start one, restore, the last deep check ([backup](BACKUP.md)) |\n| Audit | who changed what, when |\n| Calls | the call log, one row per request |\n| Keys | your key, WhoAmI, certify and register keys |\n| Settings | server, HTTPS, trusted certificates, your name |\n| Help | these guides, searchable ([in-app help](HELP.md)) |\n\nPages you may not use (by role) show the reason instead of data.\n",
  "upgrades": "# Upgrades and watchdogs\n\nThe agents and the site server upgrade themselves from owner-signed release packages\n(`itcmdr-agent-release`, `itcmdr-server-release`), with a watchdog that rolls back a bad release.\n\n## Update window\n\n`updates.auto` (default true) and `updates.window` (default `daily 01:00-04:00` in `updates.timezone`,\ndefault the server's zone). The window holds the server upgrade, agent offers and ring advances. A range\nlike `Mon-Fri 22:00-02:00` belongs to the day it starts. A bad window fails closed (nothing installs).\nChange it with `itcmdr-cli config set updates.window \"Sat,Sun 02:00-05:00\"`, the site app's Updates page,\nor `itcmdr-admin config set <site> updates.window ...`. `agent.upgrades = false` holds one machine back.\n\n## Agents: rings\n\n    itcmdr-cli plugins install --path itcmdr-agent-release-<ver>.tar.gz\n    itcmdr-cli upgrade start <ver> [--now]       # ring 0 first; --now ignores the window\n    itcmdr-cli upgrade status | pause | resume | abort\n\nA release is a service-plugin package with a `release.toml` (`version`, `min_from`), signed by an owner\nkey; it is never pushed as a plugin. Rings come from `agent.ring` (0 = a test PC, 1 = one PC,\n2 = everything). When every agent of a ring runs the new version with fresh heartbeats, the ring soaks\nfor `agent.upgradeSoakMins` and the next ring starts. A failed apply, a watchdog rollback or a new\nCritical alert on a ring machine pauses the rollout and raises a Critical alert.\n\n## The site server\n\n    itcmdr-cli upgrade server-start <ver> [--now]\n    itcmdr-cli upgrade server-abort              # while it still waits for the window\n\nThe server takes a database safety copy and stages the new files; `itcmdr-server-watchdog` swaps\nthem in and health-checks. On failure it rolls back and restores the database\n(`itcmdr-cli upgrade restore-db`) and opens a Critical `server-upgrade` alert. Agent releases that\nneed a newer server (`min_server`) wait for it.\n\n## Watchdogs\n\n`itcmdr-agent-watchdog` and `itcmdr-server-watchdog` restart their service when its health file is\nolder than `--stale-secs` (default 300) and perform the swap and rollback. `--once` runs one pass.\n\n## From adminsvr\n\nApprove a release once and roll it out to the sites you choose: `itcmdr-admin release approve|rollout`\n([adminsvr](ADMINSVR.md)). The site must allow `release.rollout` (`itcmdr-cli adminsvr allow ...`).\n\n## Installer logs (v1.2.1)\nEvery Windows installer run (new install or upgrade, interactive or `/VERYSILENT`) writes a log, also when it fails:\n- Server, agent, office app, CLI, admin CLI and admin app: `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr\\install-logs\\<part>-<version>-<yyyymmdd-hhnnss>.log`.\n- adminsvr: `%ProgramData%\\itcmdr-adminsvr\\install-logs\\`.\n\nThe log has one timestamped line per step: the version found (fresh install or \"upgrade from X\"), each service stop and the wait for STOPPED, each process exit (forced with `taskkill /F` after 30 s), a lock probe of every program file (30 tries, 2 s apart, each retry logged), files replaced, each service start and the wait for RUNNING (one `sc start` retry), the result and the exit code. Inno Setup's own log is appended under `---- Inno Setup log ----`. The command line is not logged (it may hold an enrollment token). Old logs are not pruned.\n\nA failed run is loud:\n- Exit code **7**: failed before any file was changed (a service did not stop, a file stayed locked). Exit code **8**: files installed, but a service that was running before did not start. Otherwise Inno Setup's own exit code.\n- The services that were running are started again (on the old version when nothing was replaced).\n- An Application event log entry, source `itcmdr-setup`, id 100.\n- `last-<part>.json` next to the logs: `part`, `from`, `to`, `ok`, `step`, `error`, `exit`, `log`, `time`.\n- The site server reads `last-server.json` on its next self-upgrade tick and opens the Critical alert `installer-server` (a good run resolves it); each agent reads its own `last-agent.json` at start and every minute and sends `installer-agent`. The file is then renamed to `last-<part>.reported.json`.\n- `itcmdr-cli setup check` shows `install.server` and `install.agent` (Ok, or Fail with the log path).\n\nTesting the failure path: `/SIMULATEFAIL=lock` fails at the lock probe (exit 7, nothing changed); `/SIMULATEFAIL=start` fails the service start check (exit 8).\n",
};
